tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43806:   "id": "mapledmaaddr",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43811:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nThe read arm of `case 0x04: /* MAPLE_DMAADDR */` reads, in order:\n    fatal(\"[ dreamcast_maple: TODO: read from dmaaddr ]\\n\");\n    odata = d->dmaaddr;\n    exit(1);\n**The correct value is computed and then thrown away by terminating the process.**  The WRITE arm two lines above stores `d->dmaaddr = idata', so the register is FULLY MODELLED in both directions -- a guest that writes the DMA address and reads it back, which is the ordinary way to confirm a controller programming step, is killed on the read.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_dreamcast.c adds `dreamcast_maple' unconditionally.  Nothing has to start DMA or build a command list; the plain readback reaches it.\n\n*** THIS IS THE ACCEPT MEMBER'S TEXTBOOK SHAPE, which the option set records as member 4: the model ALREADY computes the right value and then discards it, so the disposition is to delete the kill and KEEP THE VALUE, with NO latch -- there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code (an `unimplemented; ignored' message about a read that is implemented and is not ignored). ***  Two deleted lines.\n\nFAULT is not available and would be wrong anyway: memory_rw.c converts a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY, and this device sits on an SH4 machine -- but the access is implemented, so refusing it would be the wrong answer even if the machinery existed.\n\nTEST CARRIER EXISTS: regress/gdrom_default_probe.py already supplies a Dreamcast session with guest load/store helpers.  The oracle must assert that a separately STORED value reads back EXACTLY and that execution continues -- observing a zero, or a surviving debugger prompt, would be a vacuous row, since both are true of several wrong implementations.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built.  The rung-3 attempt is owed before this may be recorded documented-only.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (acceptclass/codex_acceptclass_20260910.txt): VERDICT IS **HOLD** THE TWO-LINE ACCEPT, AND IT REFUTED A PREMISE OF MINE.  Both points verified locally before recording. ***\n\n**MY PREMISE 'the correct value is computed and then thrown away' IS TOO GENEROUS.**  `odata` is a LOCAL.  The copy that delivers it -- `memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata)' -- sits at the FUNCTION TAIL, after the switch, so `exit(1)' fires first and **the value never reaches the caller at all**.  The fix is unchanged (deleting the kill lets execution reach the tail and the value IS then delivered), but the DESCRIPTION was wrong and a wrong description gets reused.\n\n**AND THE ROUND IS HELD ON A SEMANTIC QUESTION I DID NOT ASK: what should a read of the DMA address RETURN?**  Real Maple hardware may advance a DMA cursor, in which case a post-transfer readback should give the ADVANCED cursor rather than the address the guest programmed.  The model stores only what was written.  So ACCEPT-as-stored may be shipping a plausible wrong value in place of a loud stop, which is the worse outcome and is exactly the hazard the brief's own question 2 raised about the `TODO'.  **The round owes a settled hardware contract before it may ship**, and the detector's `A.afterdma' row must take its expected value FROM THAT CONTRACT rather than from today's implementation -- with transfer evidence alongside the address readback, so that skipping DMA cannot manufacture a pass.\n\n*** THE HELD QUESTION WAS AIMED AT THE WRONG ARM, AND THE TREE ANSWERS IT ITSELF.  Found 2026-09-10 by opening the file, in under two minutes, which is what the check-do-not-judge rule is for. ***\n\nThe review held this round on *what should a read of the DMA address RETURN -- the programmed address, or an ADVANCED cursor?*, and warned that ACCEPT-as-stored might ship a plausible wrong value in place of a loud stop.  **The premise under that question is that the model stores only what was written.  IT DOES NOT.**\n\n`maple_do_dma_xfer()' walks the command list accumulating `addr += datalen_cmd * 4' and then, at its tail, immediately before asserting SYSASIC_EVENT_MAPLE_DMADONE:\n\n        // TODO: Should the DMA address be updated?\n        d->dmaaddr = addr;\n\n**So the advancing cursor is ALREADY MODELLED, and the tree flags its own uncertainty about it in a comment one line above.**  A read after a transfer would return the ADVANCED address; a read before any transfer returns the programmed one.\n\n*** WHAT THAT CHANGES: THE TWO QUESTIONS ARE ON DIFFERENT SITES AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS THIS ROUND'S. ***  The read arm does not decide what `d->dmaaddr' holds -- it is a pure reporter of a field that `maple_do_dma_xfer()' owns.  Whether the advance is architecturally right is a PRE-EXISTING open question at a DIFFERENT site, which this round neither creates nor resolves, and deleting the read-side termination leaves it exactly where it was.  **Filed separately as [[mapledmaadvance]] rather than folded in here** -- the same discipline the sibling review applied to [[xscaleinten]], where a write-side kill on the adjacent case was ruled a separate row.\n\n**AND THE `plausible wrong value versus a loud stop' TRADE IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS, in two ways worth stating rather than glossing.**  The loud stop is `exit(1)' -- it ends the emulator, so nobody acts on it as a diagnostic; and the SAME unverified address is ALREADY handed to the DMA engine on every transfer, so the exposure the review is protecting against exists whether or not a guest ever reads the register back.  The read arm is the one place the value is currently NOT consumed.\n\n**THE DETECTOR CONSEQUENCE, which is the part the review was right to press on:** the after-DMA row must NOT pin the advanced value as architecturally correct, because this round has not established that.  It pins the READ ARM'S contract, which is what the diff changes -- (i) a stored value reads back exactly with no transfer in between, and (ii) the value read after a transfer DIFFERS from the programmed one, which proves the read reports the live field rather than a constant or a stale snapshot -- with transfer evidence alongside, so that a skipped DMA cannot manufacture a pass.  The advanced value's CORRECTNESS is [[mapledmaadvance]]'s to pin, and the contract paragraph says so under clause (e).\n\n*Recorded before any seat was asked, then sent for checking rather than acted on: the review that raised the hold was right that the round owed an answer, and the answer turned out to be in the file rather than in a datasheet.*\n\n*** REVIEWED 2026-09-10 (mapledmaaddr/codex_readarm_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  VERDICT: **THE READ-ARM CORRECTION MAY PROCEED INDEPENDENTLY.**  My state trace was right, my observability claim was wrong, and MY PROPOSED DETECTOR IS INSUFFICIENT. ***\n\n**THE SEPARATION HOLDS**, on a bounded basis the review stated rather than assumed: the read correction implements a separately testable rule -- *return the stored register field* -- while the cursor item decides what the transfer routine should STORE there.  The cursor policy already affects execution beyond this arm, since a subsequent transfer starts from `d->dmaaddr' and so already consumes the advanced value whether or not any guest reads it.\n\n**BUT MY FRAMING WAS TOO STRONG IN ONE PLACE, and the correction is exact:** *\"the edit does not change cursor computation\"* is correct; *\"the edit does not change the OBSERVABILITY of that computation\"* would NOT be -- a guest read that previously stopped execution can now obtain the field.  That is a new observable, and the round must say so rather than claim the diff is behaviour-preserving.\n\nTHREE CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN NOTE ABOVE, all from reading the file:\n  1. **The advance is MORE than the payload increment I quoted.**  Each descriptor advances by **8 bytes of control words + 4 bytes of command word + 4*datalen_cmd of data**, so the committed value is `C = (P + SUM(12 + 4*D_i)) mod 2^32'.  I wrote only the `4 * datalen_cmd' term.\n  2. **\"Advanced\" does not mean numerically larger** -- the sum is taken modulo 2^32, so wraparound is possible, and it is not guaranteed that `C != P'.\n  3. *** MY EARLY-EXIT CLAIM WAS WRONG. ***  I wrote that after an aborted transfer the field holds *the PROGRAMMED address*.  It holds the **ENTRY value**, which may itself be a PREVIOUS transfer's cursor -- an early return preserves what was there, it does not restore a separately retained programmed address.  Also the `!d->enable' check comes FIRST, so a disabled AND unaligned address takes the disabled return and never reaches the alignment diagnostic.\n\nWRITERS OF THE FIELD, complete for these two files: structure initialisation, the register write arm, and the DMA completion assignment.  Neither reset register clears it (`MAPLE_RESET' clears `enable'), and the tick function touches input queues only.  **The cursor is published at the TAIL, not incrementally**, and the ordinary trigger is SYNCHRONOUS -- writing 1 to `MAPLE_STATE' calls the transfer routine directly, with no scheduled DMA worker -- so no partially-advanced value can be observed.  *Scope caveat the seat raised itself: the routine calls `cpu->memory_rw', and these two files do not establish that such a callback can never re-enter device access.*\n\n*** THE DETECTOR I PROPOSED IS INSUFFICIENT, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT CHANGES THE ROUND. ***\n  * **Assertion (i), exact readback with no DMA, is passable by four wrong implementations:** a constant equal to the one test value; constant zero against an initial-zero-only test; ALIGNMENT MASKING against any test using only aligned values; and a separate snapshot of the last programmed address that never tracks DMA at all.  *Remedy: several distinct values, including nonzero HIGH bits and nonzero LOW FIVE bits, and successive writes shown to replace what reads report.  No DMA is needed for the unaligned cases.*\n  * **Assertion (ii), post-transfer inequality, proves only that the answer differs from `P'** -- satisfied by zero, by a fabricated `P + 4', by a stale cursor from an earlier transfer, and by any masked transform that still differs.  Independent DMA evidence rules out a SKIPPED transfer; it says nothing about the relationship between the returned value and the field.\n  * **THE FORM THAT WORKS:** capture the actual field value `S' immediately before the read, then assert the delivered register value equals `S' EXACTLY, that the read leaves the field unchanged, and that the access returns normally with NO read-side diagnostic.  Apply it both after ordinary programming and after an independently demonstrated transfer.\n  * *** `MAPLE_STATE == 0' IS NOT DMA EVIDENCE: its read arm always returns zero, including after an early return. ***  Use a supported command and verify a fresh expected response in a receive buffer pre-filled with distinguishable contents; a completion event alone is weaker, because the descriptor loop can reach the tail through paths that do not implement the request fully.\n  * **Candidate (c) -- keep `fatal()' and delete only `exit(1)' -- is NOT rejected by any value assertion if `fatal()' returns.  Silence and normal completion must be checked separately.**\n\nPARTITION OF THE CHANGE, exact: only `relative_addr == 0x04' in the non-write branch moves.  The changed read class includes EVERY field value and history -- initial zero, freshly programmed values, UNALIGNED values, completed-DMA cursors, values preserved by disabled or unaligned attempts, and values retained across reset writes -- with no enable or alignment condition on the corrected read.  *A syntactic subtlety worth a row's comment: the `else' also covers a direction flag equal to neither MEM_WRITE nor MEM_READ, which previously terminated and afterwards assigns `odata' and returns without serialising it.*\n\nAttachment is unconditional at `machine_dreamcast.c:149', BEFORE the `prom_emulation' early return.\n\n*** A 'SETTLED PREMISE' I GAVE THE PANEL WAS A FACT ABOUT A DIFFERENT DEVICE.  Corrected 2026-09-11 (mapledmaaddr/codex_detector_20260911.txt), verified here by opening the file. ***\n\nI wrote, as settled: *\"`odata` is computed BEFORE the offset is examined, and delivered by the common tail, so an 'ignored' read still returns the value.\"*  **That is true of `dev_wdc.c`'s alt-status handler and FALSE of this one.**  `dev_dreamcast_maple.c` initialises `odata = 0` at `:550`, assigns `odata = d->dmaaddr` **INSIDE** the `0x04` read arm, and delivers at `:638`.\n\n**THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC: for Maple an ignored read returns ZERO, not the field.**  So the *decline-in-place* option here is **not** equivalent to *change nothing*, which is exactly what it WAS for the WDC row -- and I had carried that equivalence across as though it were a property of the pattern rather than of that file.\n\n*** THIS IS THE FOURTH SCOPE-TRANSFER ERROR OF THE SESSION AND THEY ARE ALL ONE SHAPE: A CLAIM VERIFIED IN ONE SCOPE, REUSED IN ANOTHER WITHOUT RE-CHECKING. ***\n  1. `#519`: the clause-(d) flow test applied correctly to `tlb_mode`, then **stopped at the array and never reached the index** -- six escapes, round re-opened.\n  2. `#519`: the `color_mode` warrant true of the arm I edited, **false of its sibling arms**.\n  3. `rlwinm`: `rlwimi`'s ordering warrant **did not transfer one call earlier**, into the decoder -- the adopted repair was inert.\n  4. here: a WDC handler's control flow **transferred to a Maple handler** that does not share it.\n\n**Each was caught by a seat or by reading, never by re-reading my own reasoning** -- which is the argument for the premise-audit opener and for naming premises as QUESTIONS rather than as settled facts.  *A premise inherited from a sibling investigation is the most dangerous kind, because it arrives already believed.*\n\n**THE DETECTOR IS VIABLE: it can distinguish all six states**, provided the read-value, DMA-response, execution and diagnostic assertions are kept **separate** rather than folded into one row.  The strict oracle is *the guest's returned 32-bit value equals an INDEPENDENTLY OBSERVED current `d->dmaaddr`* -- obtained by host-side inspection immediately before the read, and **not derived from the same path under test.**\n\n*** 2026-09-11: A SEVENTH CAUSE OF A ZERO-BYTE CODEX ANSWER: TRANSPORT, AND IT DEMANDS THE OPPOSITE RESPONSE FROM THE ONE THE RECORD WOULD HAVE GIVEN IT. ***\n\n`regress/artifacts/mapledmaaddr/codex_detector_20260911.txt` is ZERO BYTES with a 213 KB `.err`.  The staging review was about to file it under the two-refusals rule -- *\"if it refuses twice, commit both zero-byte files as the record and stop\"*.  **It never refused.**  Its `.err` ends:\n\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 2/5\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 3/5\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 4/5\n\nand the content immediately before that is ordinary substantive analysis, mid-sentence, of the probe's `dma alias` row.  **The seat was answering and the connection died under it.**\n\nTHE SIX CAUSES ALREADY ON RECORD -- terminal refusal, quota wall, still in flight, cp1252 decode death, auth revocation, and `rc=-1` with the answer in the `.err` -- **none fits.**  There is no cyber flag at the tail, no quota text, the process exited, the decode is fine, auth is live (other codex jobs answered in the same window), and `rc` was 0.\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE `.err` TAIL: `Reconnecting... N/5` MEANS TRANSPORT, AND A TRANSPORT FAILURE IS RE-FIRED IMMEDIATELY.**  A refusal is the seat declining the SUBJECT, so re-firing the same brief buys nothing and the two-refusals rule exists to stop you paying twice for it.  A transport drop is content-independent -- *the seat had no opinion about the brief at all* -- so re-firing is the correct and only response.\n\n**WHY THE MISFILING WOULD HAVE BEEN EXPENSIVE RATHER THAN UNTIDY:** filing it as a refusal would have recorded *\"codex declines the Maple subject\"* -- a durable, false fact about a SEAT, of exactly the kind this project has repeatedly had to un-learn (\"it was never the seat, it was the brief\", the kimi substitute left unfired for days, minimax written off for a week).  **A seat wrongly recorded as refusing a subject is a seat nobody fires again.**\n\n*This is the daemon-rule's shape one transport up: a WALL is the seat refusing and you wait it out; a REFUSED CONNECTION is the transport being absent and one command fixes it.  Codex has now shown the second kind, and its `.err` says so in as many words -- which is why the standing rule is CHECK THE `.err`, NOT THE SIZE.*\n\nRe-fired as `codex_detector_RETRY_20260911`.  **Both files are kept**: 21 zero-byte codex answers are already tracked, all `codex_*`, including a committed failed-retry PAIR at `crimewidthkill/codex_witness_20260910.txt` + `..._RETRY_...`.  The zero-byte file IS the record of the loss, and now carries its cause.\n\n*** THE TRANSPORT CLASS ABOVE IS REFUTED BY ITS OWN INSTANCE.  I WROTE IT HOURS AGO AND THE ANSWER LANDED COMPLETE. ***\n\n`codex_detector_20260911.txt` is **16,624 bytes**, carries its liveness token, and ends in a finished sentence.  **It was never lost.**  And the `Reconnecting... N/5` lines I keyed on sit at `.err` lines **3821-3823 of 3976** -- *155 lines from the end, not at the tail*.  The CLI reconnected and carried on.\n\n**SO THE DIAGNOSIS WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS: the seat had an opinion about the brief, delivered it, and I recorded that it had none.**  The zero bytes I read were the NORMAL MID-RUN STATE of a direct `codex exec` -- whose shell creates the redirect target at launch, so **0 bytes means started-and-running**, which is the discriminator I had written into `codexfarm.py` myself earlier the same session.\n\n> *** THIS IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY I READ A MID-RUN FILE AS A DEAD JOB, AND THE FIRST TIME COST A COMPLETED 12,032-BYTE ANSWER. ***  The structural fix I built covers `codexfarm` jobs, which now carry a `.RUNNING` sentinel.  **This job was a direct `codex exec` with a shell redirect and has no sentinel** -- so the fix did not reach the case, and I applied the wrong rule again to the one shape it does not cover.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, and it is narrow:** a genuinely dead transport is still distinguishable, and the `codexfarm.py` check I added tests the **LAST EIGHT LINES** plus an empty answer.  Here the reconnects are 155 lines from the end, so **that check would correctly NOT have fired** -- it is narrower than the prose I wrote around it, and the prose was the defective half.\n\n**THE CORRECTED RULE: `Reconnecting... N/5` ANYWHERE IN A `.err` MEANS THE CLI HIT NETWORK TROUBLE, NOT THAT THE JOB DIED.**  It is diagnostic of nothing on its own.  What decides liveness is the same thing it always was: **the job has not notified yet** -- the harness tracks background jobs and reports on exit, and that signal was available and unconsulted both times.\n\n*A correction is not safer than the thing it corrects: this one minted a class from a single instance, exactly as this project's own freshly-minted-exemplar rule warns, and the instance refuted it four minutes later.  The retry fired at a DIFFERENT path, so nothing was clobbered -- the path discipline held where the reasoning did not, and the retry is kept as an independent second answer on one brief rather than cancelled.*\n\n**THE ANSWER ITSELF REPORTS A FINDING WORTH THE ROUND:** *\"A detector can separate the six states, but one 'settled' premise is false in the specified source.\"*  Unharvested; that is the next step on this row.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **CORRECTING THE TEMPLATE DOES NOT CORRECT THE BRIEFS ALREADY ON DISK**, AND I FIRED THE UNCORRECTED ONE TWICE TODAY. ***\n\nThe brief's settled-premises block said *\"`odata` is computed BEFORE the offset is examined, and delivered by the common tail (`if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;`), so an 'ignored' read still returns the value.\"*  **True of `dev_wdc.c`.  FALSE of this device.**\n\n`dev_dreamcast_maple.c:550` initialises `uint64_t idata = 0, odata = 0;`; the `0x04` arm assigns inside itself; the value returns through `memory_writemax64()` at `:637`.  **There is no `data[0] = odata` tail here, so deleting the assignment returns ZERO rather than the live value** -- which inverts what the oracle must look for.  Verified by opening the file.\n\n*** THE CHAIN IS THE FINDING, AND IT IS NEW. ***\n  1. The error was made -- a premise carried from `dev_wdc.c` to a device that does not share it.\n  2. It was CAUGHT, and recorded as **instance #4 in `tools/BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`'s scope-transfer table (`:253`), naming this exact device and this exact premise.**\n  3. **The BRIEF was never corrected.**\n  4. I fired that brief today.  Twice -- the original and a retry.\n  5. The seat caught the same error again, independently, from the source.\n\n> **A TEMPLATE GOVERNS BRIEFS WRITTEN *FROM* IT.  IT DOES NOT REACH BRIEFS ALREADY WRITTEN.**  Recording a scope-transfer instance in the template closes the class for FUTURE briefs and leaves the originating brief on disk, loaded, refutable, and re-fireable.\n\n*This is the carrier's own thesis landing on the one artifact it holds up as the piece of prose that demonstrably works: the premise-audit block fires because it lives where the decision happens.  It does -- for a brief being WRITTEN.  The decision to RE-FIRE an existing brief happens somewhere else entirely, and nothing was watching there.*\n\n**REMEDY APPLIED: the brief is corrected IN PLACE, quoting what it refutes** (the retry now running had already read the old text, so this stops the third firing rather than the second).  **The general form -- when a scope-transfer instance is recorded in the template, correct the brief it came from in the same edit -- is a one-line habit and belongs in the template's own §C block.**\n\nAND THE ANSWER'S DESIGN CONTENT STANDS, with four vacuity findings stated against NAMED mutants rather than universally: post-transfer inequality is **insufficient** (many wrong values satisfy it); the unaligned-disabled retry is **vacuous for enable-guard deletion** (alignment refusal preserves the same entry value); **silence alone is vacuous** (no execution also produces no diagnostic); and a fresh expected response is **vacuous for a read-arm-only value mutant** (it establishes DMA execution; the address row carries the value rejection).  Weakenings: (a) DIFFERENT, (c) DIFFERENT, (d) DIFFERENT, and **(b) EQUIVALENT for the `0x04` contract but DIFFERENT for the whole callback** -- `W(04,1); R(10)` returns 0 originally and 1 after the hoist, caught by an `other zero` row.\n\n*Method note: my first grep for the premise in the brief returned ZERO and I nearly reported the brief clean -- the text is `` `odata` is computed `` and the backticks broke the pattern.  `grep -F` with a denominator found it at lines 39-40.  The pattern-that-matched-nothing trap, inside the check for a different error.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **THE RETRY REPLICATES THE REFUTATION INDEPENDENTLY (badge `s`)**. ***\n\nThe re-fire (18,024 b, `codex_detector_RETRY_20260911.txt`) reaches the same verdict from the same three files in a separate invocation: *\"The two-line fix is sound.  A strong detector can compare readback with an independent observation of the live field, without endorsing the cursor arithmetic.  **One 'settled' premise is false in this checkout.**\"*\n\n**So my `odata` premise is refuted TWICE, independently.**  Badged `s` -- SAME-MODEL replication, which this project's doctrine ranks below two-family agreement and forbids passing off as it.  *It is still worth more than one answer: the two runs did not share a process, and the second was fired before I had corrected the brief, so both read the identical defective packet and both caught it.*\n\n**AND THE RETRY ANSWERS THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED:** the detector can pin the read arm's contract **by comparing readback against an INDEPENDENT observation of the live field**, *\"without endorsing the cursor arithmetic\"* -- i.e. without certifying that the advanced value is architecturally right, which is a separate open question with no local documentation.  That is exactly the separation the contract paragraph needs.\n\n*The duplicate firing was caused by my own misdiagnosis and cost one extra invocation.  Recorded honestly: it bought a replication, but it was not bought deliberately, and a replication chosen on purpose would have gone to a DIFFERENT family.*",
