tools/pipeline/ledger.json:44238:   "commits": "ca3be4e"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:44246:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the first brief was REFUSED by the codex cybersecurity filter; re-worded as plain mutation testing it answered (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_promote_20260912b.txt).  regress/xscale_inten_probe.py CREATED from the witness + 16 codex replacements (D_OP1, INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER, BLOCKED reporting, stale-prompt and ignored-timeout fixes, RESULT/CONTROL lines) + D_CRM1_TMR0, the crm-isolation row codex said remained open (tmr0 <- 0x5A5A0000, TMRx_ENABLE clear).  29 rows, UNMEASURED.  The gate_arm.sh section is designed, NOT applied.  MANIFEST entry owed. ***\n\n*** PASS 2, 2026-09-11 (GPT-6 Astra, effort max): **A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IS PREDICTED, NOT YET\nMEASURED.**  THE ROUND IS NOT SHIPPABLE UNTIL IT IS BUILT AND SCORED. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_pass2_20260911.txt\n\nVerdict: *\"The detector has holes: an opcode1-gated reinstatement escapes, and several recorded\nkills are misattributed. I found no reason to change the deletion itself.\"*\n\n**THE ESCAPING MUTANT (`M_OPCODE1`), inside the existing inten load arm, after the assignment:**\n    if (opcode1 != 0) { fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\"); exit(1); }\n\nIt keeps the enclosing predicates and introduces a DIFFERENT one, which is exactly the clause-(d)\nform a PURE DELETION owes -- not the original death condition widened.  **The seat DECODED THE\nLITERAL CP6 INSTRUCTION WORDS in the probe** and found every tested inten access carries\n`opcode1 == 0`; the only nonzero-opcode1 instruction is `E_UNKNOWN` (`opcode1=3, crn=9, crm=2`),\nwhich never reaches the edited arm and dies through `unknown` anyway.  So it predicts **26/26, a\nTOTAL ESCAPE**, while a guest issuing an inten read with nonzero opcode1 would be killed -- so it is\nNOT an equivalent mutant.\n\n*** THAT 26/26 IS A PREDICTION WRITTEN IN THE MEASURED COLUMN'S CHAIR, AND THIS PROJECT HAS PAID\nFOR THAT FOUR TIMES.  IT IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  The leg must be BUILT and RUN\nbefore the breach is graded L2 -- deferred only because a gate held the build trees when it was\nfound.  If it escapes, it is **L2: replace IN-ROUND however late**.\n\n**THE PRETABLE'S clause-(e) DISCLOSURE DOES NOT COVER IT.**  It discloses `opcode1=3` as *\"tested in\narm E\"* -- but arm E tests that value in an ALREADY-REJECTED register tuple, which says nothing\nabout opcode1 at `crm=0, crn=0`.  **A disclosure that names the right VALUE at the wrong SITE is not\na disclosure of the cell.**  The PRETABLE's GUARD FIELD list is also wrong to call opcode1/opcode2\nguards: they are function INPUTS that this function never dispatches on.\n\nTWO MORE ROWS OWED, both cheap and both from the same pass:\n  * **`INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER`** -- write isteer=0, write inten=S1, read isteer back and\n    expect 0.  A SECOND anchored row against the M5 register substitution, independent of the\n    free-running IRQ path.  **Today `ANCHOR_EXC` is the SOLE discriminator for M5 (25/26) and it is\n    the suite's most load-sensitive row** -- breakpoint plus timeout plus free-run.\n  * **a stepped `tmr0` read at `crm=1, crn=0, l_bit=1`** -- the de-escalation accept row for the\n    `crm` predicate IN ISOLATION.  `D_TRR0` changes crm AND crn together, so it does not hold the\n    other former gates true.  tmr0 is chosen because its read has none of tcr0/tcr1's\n    counter-increment side effect.\n\nMY M5 READING WAS UPHELD WITH TWO CORRECTIONS WORTH KEEPING: **not every `D_*` row is a round-trip**\n(`D_RESET` reads before any inten write and `D_TRR0` exercises another register -- `D_RESET` merely\nfails to discriminate because the reset values coincide), and *\"one discriminator for this\nconsistent M5 substitution\"* is NARROWER than *\"one row capable of detecting any wrong-register\nedit\"*.  *A locally-true restatement that widens a claim is the shape this project has recorded as\nharder to catch than a plain wrong answer.*\n\nTHE FIX ITSELF IS UNCHANGED AND ENDORSED: *\"I would keep the fix exactly as supplied\"*, and ACCEPT\n(option-set member 4) is upheld -- *\"I cannot justify a once-only announcement for the implemented\nread.\"*  **Eight passes on this round's detector, none on its fix until now, and the first pass on\nthe fix approved it while finding the detector's enumeration incomplete.**\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 L4 RECORD CORRECTION -- THIS ROW READ `state: open' WITH NO WITNESS FIELD WHILE A\nTWO-COLOUR RUNG-3 WITNESS, A PRETABLE.out AND A GREEN SEVEN-LEG KILL TABLE SAT IN ITS OWN\nARTIFACTS DIRECTORY. ***\n\nI wrote `xscale -- no round run' into the checkpoint this afternoon and it was FALSE.  Found by\nopening `regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/', not by any check.  What is measured there:\n\n    BASE       26/26  FAILING: NONE          M_OPCODE2  19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    HEAD       11/26  (15 rows red)          M_RD15     25/26  (RD15_SURVIVE)\n    M1         23/26  (3 ANCHOR rows red)    M_NREAD    19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    M5         25/26  (ANCHOR_EXC)           RESTORED   26/26  FAILING: NONE\n\ncited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt.  **Floor clause (a) is met\n(HEAD red), every mutant is dead on its intended rows, and the restore verified BOTH parts --\nsource cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN and binary FRESH -- before clearing the sentinel.**\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR, because the first draft of this correction overstated the class and the count\nrefuted it: 169 ledger rows sit in work/held/open with zero entries, which is the NORMAL state of\na filed row.  Testing each for an artifacts dir holding a witness or killtable narrows it to\n**FOUR** -- ppcrldimimask, ppcrlwimiwidth, wdcaltdigin and this row -- and the other three are\nrecorded CORRECTLY, `in work' with a witness field naming the artifact.  *The records practice is\nholding; this row was the sole outlier, and a systemic claim would have been wrong.*\n\nSTILL OWED and NOT discharged by this edit: the round has had THREE seats (codex x2 2026-09-10,\nagy x1 2026-09-11) against a standing NINE-SEAT directive, and no panel dir cites it.  The fix is\nuncommitted (` M src/cpus/cpu_arm_coproc.c'), byte-identical in est/, p1=CLEAN p2=FRESH in all\nthree build trees.  **HELD for the panel under the owner's hold-everything ruling -- this edit\ncorrects the RECORD only and licenses nothing.**\n\n---\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one.\n\n*** THE OTHER SEVEN EXITS IN THIS FILE ARE TRIAGED, 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_coproc_20260910.txt, ZERO cyber flags) -- AND MY WORKING HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. ***\n\nI asked which of the other seven share this row's shape, on the theory that 'the value is already computed and then discarded' would make several of them one cheap round.  **NONE OF THE OTHER SEVEN HAS THE SAME WARRANTED TWO-LINE ACCEPT REPAIR.**  That is a useful negative: it stops a round copying this disposition across the file, which is exactly what the option-set rule warns about when a precedent is transferred without checking each site.\n\n**AND THE CENSUS UNDERCOUNTED THE FILE: eight ACTIVE exits plus SIX COMMENTED-OUT ones.**  The commented six are inert and are not defects, but they are worth knowing about -- a round de-fatalising a live site sits beside six places where someone already made that call and left the corpse in.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE, which is what decides how the remaining seven split into rounds: three CP15 sites, four CP6, one CP14 -- or by SHAPE, five specific-case sites, TWO DEFAULT-FED ENDPOINTS and one ENTRY GUARD.  The two default-fed ones are the priorities: CP6's shared `unknown:' label is reached from THREE defaults (the outer crm switch and both nested crn switches) and CP14's from FOUR plus a separate opcode2 guard -- so one repair at each label covers many decode paths at once.\n\n*** ONE SITE IS NEITHER A CASE NOR A DEFAULT, which no other exit in this queue has been: CP15's `rd == ARM_PC' ENTRY GUARD, before the switch, in BOTH directions. ***  A refusal that fires before any dispatch is a different fix shape from every other member of this class, and it is ranked last for that reason rather than because it is unimportant.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ANSWERED **YES** 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_witness_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND MY NEGATIVE PREMISE WAS FALSE.  This row now has a ratified disposition, a confirmed path, and a named vehicle -- it needs a measuring seat and nothing else. ***\n\n**INSTALLERS: exactly two, and both assign BEFORE the `!machine->prom_emulation' early return**, so disabling PROM emulation does not remove CP6 -- `machine_iq80321.c' (default CPU `80321_600_2') and `machine_iyonix.c' (`80321_600_B0'), each a direct `cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = ...'.  No other installer or caller exists.  **Selecting an XScale CPU is NOT enough**: generic ARM init installs CP15 and, for XScale, CP14 -- never CP6.  Note the setup receives the BOOTSTRAP cpu only.\n\n**PATH:** guest fetch -> `arm_instr_to_be_translated' -> condition-selected `arm_instr_mcr_mrc' -> `arm_mcr_mrc' -> `coproc[6]' -> this handler.  **No privilege or mode check anywhere in that chain** -- dispatch does not consult CPAR (stored and read through CP15, and the source says it is otherwise unused).  Cold init already selects SVC32 with IRQ/FIQ masked, so no privilege transition is needed.  Prerequisites are ordinary: the condition must pass, the fetch must succeed, ARM state not Thumb, and CP6 must be installed -- with a null entry `arm_mcr_mrc' prints a generic TODO and **its undefined-exception call and `exit(1)' are COMMENTED OUT**, so absence does not terminate.\n\n*** MY NEGATIVE PREMISE -- 'the machinery is store/load-oriented and nothing drives a coprocessor transfer' -- IS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  FIVE existing probes already execute CP15 MRC/MCR on `cats': `footbridge_sites_probe.py', `fbpending_witness.py', `fbpending_drain_probe.py', `pit8253_latch_probe.py', and gate 14's `arm_flags_probe.py' on `testarm'.  **The directly reusable pattern is `footbridge_sites_probe.py'** -- launch a stock machine into the debugger, plant instructions in RAM, set pc, step, check disassembly, inspect registers -- pointed at IQ80321 or Iyonix instead of `cats'.  **No machine or device addition is required**, which is what the witness ladder demands.  No dedicated CP6 test exists yet.\n\n*** AN ORACLE TRAP THAT APPLIES FAR BEYOND THIS ROW: A PRINTED STEP DISASSEMBLY DOES NOT PROVE THE INSTRUCTION EXECUTED, OR EVEN THAT ITS CONDITION PASSED.  The engine prints the disassembly BEFORE executing. ***  Any probe here that treats a stepped disassembly line as evidence of execution is asserting something the engine does not promise.\n\n**A NAIVE `write W, read, expect W' HAS FOUR INDISTINGUISHABLE FAILURES:** always-zero (passes when `W' is zero); a stale value equal to `W'; a read that never executes (passes when the destination already holds `W' -- *especially easy if the write-source register is reused as the read destination*); and a program that never ran at all, when the inspected register or memory already holds the expected value.  **The discriminating form:** two DISTINCT NONZERO values written and read in succession, the read destination deliberately pre-set to a third value before each read, and SEPARATE write-source and read-destination registers.  Plus execution evidence -- intended address, decoded coprocessor and register fields, the destination TRANSITION, and continuation -- with missing observations failing rather than defaulting to zero.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE NEEDS CONTINUATION AND SILENCE, not just a value:** the CURRENT code already assigns the destination before terminating, so the assignment alone is not the outcome under test.  Unchanged source predicts the named diagnostic and exit status 1; the corrected behaviour requires the right value, continued execution, and NO diagnostic from this read arm.\n\n**THE SAME VEHICLE REACHES EVERY OTHER TERMINATION IN THIS FUNCTION** -- interrupt steering (`crn=4', write, nonzero), interrupt acknowledge (`crn=8', write, ANY value including zero), and the shared `unknown:' label (either direction, unsupported `crn' or `crm').  `opcode1' and `opcode2' are NOT validated, so changing them alone does not route a recognised selection to `unknown'.  *** EACH SITE NEEDS ITS OWN PROCESS INVOCATION, because the first `exit(1)' reached prevents observing any later one. ***  That groups the COVERAGE work without implying the repairs should be identical.\n\n**A COUNT IN MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG: `crm=1' has SIXTEEN implemented direction arms -- eight reads and eight writes -- not fourteen.**  All sixteen are locally quiet, but *** 'quiet' DOES NOT MEAN 'a plain storage assignment' ***: `tcr0' and `tcr1' INCREMENT before returning their values, and a `tisr' write clears selected bits and deasserts timer interrupts.  A sibling-parity argument resting on 'the timer arms are plain accessors' would be wrong.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: WITNESS DESIGNED (rung 3, `-E iq80321`, six invocations).  Two of my stated premises were WRONG and the seat corrected both; one architectural fact it established changes what the round may CLAIM. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER CANNOT REACH CP6 STATE AT ALL, so clause (f)'s anchor rule cannot be satisfied in its usual form here.**  `debugger_cmd_reg()` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 -- coproc 6 cannot even be asked for; `arm_cpu_register_dump()` prints GPRs, cpsr, banked registers and the CP15 control block and **names no CP6 field anywhere**; and the settings tree carries only `pc`, `r0`-`r9`/`sl`/`fp`/`ip`/`sp`/`lr` and `cpsr`, so `print` cannot reach it either.\n\n> **The ONLY way to observe `cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten` is to make a guest MRC copy it into a GPR -- which is the instruction under test.  There is no channel OUTSIDE the decode under test.**  The three substitutes (the MCR store arm at `:315` as a different branch naming the same lvalue; the `unknown:` printf anchoring the ENCODING; the crm 1 `trr0` bank anchoring BANK SELECTION) are each real and **none is decode-independent.  The contract must say so rather than claim an anchor it does not have.**\n\nMY WRONG PREMISES, both corrected by reading: (1) I said five existing probes execute CP15 MRC/MCR **on `cats`** -- `arm_flags_probe.py` is `-E testarm`, and its CP15 word `0xEE070F9A` is an MCR that ARMS A COMBINER, not a register read.  The substance held; the machine attribution did not.  (2) I proposed `cats` as reachable -- it is not: generic ARM init installs only CP15/CP14, so `arm_mcr_mrc` takes its `else` branch, **prints and does NOT exit**, leaving the poison unchanged and the step line disassembling a perfectly good `mrc 6,...`.  *That state is pixel-identical to 'the instruction did not execute', which is why row A2 asserts the ABSENCE of `[ TODO: arm_mcr_mrc:` with `cp_num=6`.  Without it the two conflate.*\n\nTHE BLIND SPOT I FLAGGED IS ANSWERED BY A ROW, NOT A COMMENT.  **P2** asserts, from the step-PRE snapshot, that each subject destination still holds its POISON immediately before its own step; if any already holds the expected value the probe prints `VOID` for every value row and goes RED.  **X4** is its inequality companion: arm N (subject word replaced by a NOP) must NOT show `S1` in r3 -- if it does, something other than the instruction under test put it there and every value row is worthless.\n\nTHE DISASSEMBLY GAP IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY ARGUMENT.  `cpu_dyntrans.c:282-296` re-reads and disassembles the word, and executes it afterwards at `:325`; the print path never consults the condition code.  So **arm C** plants `0x0E103610` -- ONE NIBBLE from the subject word -- and the step line prints `mrceq 6,0,r3,cr0,cr0,0` while r3 must NOT change.  *One word, one nibble apart, opposite answers: that is the statement that a printed CP6 MRC and an executed one are different events.*\n\nENCODING, derived twice rather than recalled: `MRC p6,0,r3,c0,c0,0` = **0xEE103610**, from ARM ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.108 (`ddi0406.txt:25478`) AND from the emulator's own field extractor at `cpu_arm.c:2645-2651`.  The two agree field for field.  General form `0xEE100610 | (crn<<16) | (rd<<12) | crm`.\n\nSIX INVOCATIONS, and the rule is stated rather than assumed: **one per terminating site, plus one per mutually-exclusive content of the subject slot.**  Arms E (`unknown:` at `:419`) and S (isrc write at `:333`) terminate in BOTH builds, so neither can share a process with anything downstream; D and L terminate pre-fix only; N and C are the same slot with different words.  **S is the blast-radius control: if it ever goes green the deletion was not scoped to one arm, and that is a FAIL.**\n\nFOUR OF SIX ARMS MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS THE TWO BUILDS -- itself a measured statement of the diff's blast radius.\n\n(d) NULL-REPAIR: the fix adds no predicate, so the mutants reroute existing ones.  `if (l_bit)` -> `if (l_bit && cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten)` is caught by **V0 alone**, the reset read taken while the field is still 0 -- which is why V0 is in the program and was not dropped for brevity.  A kill re-added on a narrow `rd` is caught by the five distinct subject `rd` values {3,4,5,6,9}, `rd` being a floor axis because it indexes the guarded body's own lvalue.\n\nDISCLOSED (clause (e)): predicates on `opcode1`/`opcode2` -- every word here has both at 0, and the body never TESTS them (it only passes them to the `unknown:` printf), so they are not floor axes here.  Also disclosed: interrupt DELIVERY is unreachable from a stepping probe (`debugger.c:800`, `:835-840` stop the host timer), so this is necessarily a REGISTER-TRANSFER detector, not a behaviour detector.  And the `isteer`-nonzero kill at `:324` is the one remaining `crm 0` kill this detector does not observe.\n\nDISPOSITION UNCHANGED AND STRENGTHENED.  `:311` already computes the correct value before killing, and `dev_i80321.c:445-446` aliases the field into the interrupt controller (`d->enable`), read at `:86`/`:92` -- so it is live state a consumer reads, and an *\"unimplemented; ignored\"* message would be false in product code.  The sibling grep agrees: within `crm 0` the `crn 4` READ at `:318` is already a silent accept and the `crm 1` bank is silent both ways, so silencing `crn 0` makes the file MORE internally consistent.\n\nNO MMU PROLOGUE AND NO ARM T, deliberately: a coprocessor move touches no memory, so the only access is the instruction fetch at VA 0x8000, which `arm_setup_initial_translation_table(cpu, 0x4000)` maps identically.  **The probe header must say this in those words** -- a reader who knows `footbridge_sites_probe.py` will look for its arm T and must not conclude it was forgotten.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ADVERSARIAL PASS 1.  **THE ANCHOR ADMISSION IS HALF FALSE, AND FINDING THE MISSED CHANNEL CONVERTS A DISCLOSED WEAKNESS INTO A CLOSED ONE.**  One FLOOR BREACH (L2) and one TOTAL ESCAPE were found; all seven encodings verified correct. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER HALF IS CONFIRMED, line by line:** `debugger_cmds.c:1662` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 (`reg ,6` is refused); `cpu_arm.c:193-198` registers only `pc`, the GPRs and `cpsr`; `arm_cpu_register_dump` names no CP6 field; and `i80321_inten` appears in exactly three places in `src/` -- `cpu_arm_coproc.c:311,315`, `dev_i80321.c:446`, `cpu_arm.h:212`.  `DEVICE_ACCESS(i80321)` never touches `d->enable`, so there is **no MMIO alias**.\n\n*** BUT \"NO CHANNEL OUTSIDE THE DECODE UNDER TEST\" IS FALSE. ***\n\n    dev_i80321.c:446    d->enable = &cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    dev_i80321.c:84-87  { *d->status |= linemask;\n                          if (*d->status & *d->enable) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }\n\n**`i80321_assert()` is a SECOND READER of the exact cell, outside the CP6 decode, and its observable is the ARM IRQ line.  That is clause (f)'s \"side effect\" anchor, verbatim** -- and it is reachable SYNCHRONOUSLY inside one guest store: `machine_iq80321.c:61-64` wires the ns16550 as `irq=...i80321.28`, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` calls `dev_ns16550_tick()` inside the guest's own store for `com_ier` and `com_mcr`.  So `strb 0x02 -> 0xfe800001` then `strb 0x08 -> 0xfe800004` asserts line 28 and the `isrc & inten` test fires.\n\n**THE COST, stated honestly: the anchor arm must FREE-RUN.**  `cpu_dyntrans.c:205-209` suppresses the exception under `single_step`, and `irq_asserted` is in neither the `reg` dump nor the settings tree, so a stepping arm cannot see it.  Use the project's existing free-running shape: clear CPSR I, plant `b .`, breakpoint at **0x18** (`ARM_EXCEPTION_IRQ = 6`, and `ARM_CONTROL_V` is not in the reset set), `continue`.  Contrast row: `inten = 0` gives no exception and the `b .` spins to the stall budget.\n\n=== M1 -- A FLOOR BREACH (L2), AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED ===\n\n    if (l_bit) { r[rd] = inten;\n                 if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc != 0) { fatal(...); exit(1); } }\n\n**`i80321_isrc` is 0 in every one of the six arms** -- nothing asserts an i80321 interrupt in a bare probe, and the only guest-reachable writer of `isrc` is the crn-8 MCR, which `exit(1)`s.  **On a real guest it is NEVER 0 at the moment INTCTL is read, because NetBSD reads INTCTL from inside the interrupt path.**  The host dies exactly as before and nothing in the six arms can see it.\n\nGraded L2 rather than clause-(e) debt under the pass-11 generating-set ruling: `inten`'s ONLY consumer is `i80321_assert`/`i80321_deassert`, which reads `*d->status & *d->enable`, so `isrc` and `inten` are a JOINT INPUT of the model, written by different paths.  The floor is `{l_bit} x {inten classes} x {isrc = 0, != 0}` and **the whole `isrc != 0` column is empty.**  *The same anchor arm closes it: asserting the serial gives `isrc = 1<<28`, readable back through `MRC p6,0,Rt,c8,c0,0` = `0xEE187610` -- the crn-8 LOAD has no `exit(1)` (`:328-329`), a fact the design did not use.*\n\n=== M5 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE, AND IT IS NOT A HOST KILL, WHICH MAKES IT WORSE ===\n\nSwap the cell on BOTH sides to `i80321_isteer`: reset read 0, three round-trips, L, N, C, E and S all GREEN -- **while the device's interrupt enable mask is never written and a guest's interrupts silently never fire.**  The design's own anchor admission conceded this was invisible; with the channel above found, it is closable.\n\n=== THREE MORE ESCAPES, EACH CLOSED BY ONE WORD ===\n* **M2 -- the detector's real bound is FOUR READS PER PROCESS.**  Arm D performs exactly four subject reads and L performs one, each in a fresh process, so a `static int nread; if (++nread > 4) exit(1);` escapes 6/6 while a guest polling INTCTL dies.  Repair: one more read in arm D.\n* **M3 -- the `rd` axis is not closed, and the manual says WHICH value matters.**  DDI 0406 A8.8.108: *\"`<Rt>` ... can be R0-R14 or, in some cases, APSR_nzcv\"* -- **Rt = R15 means write the CONDITION FLAGS**, and GXemul writes `r[15]`, the PC, unconditionally.  That is a real modelling defect, so a future `if (rd == ARM_PC) exit(1)` is likely.  `{3,4,5,6,9}` is five of sixteen and **omits the one value with an architectural special case**.  `0xEE10F610`.\n* **M4 -- `opcode1`/`opcode2` are ignored by the arm and CONSTANT across all six invocations.**  `if (opcode2 != 0) exit(1)` escapes 6/6 and re-opens the kill for 7/8 of the encoding space.  Close with `0xEE10A630`.\n\n=== CORRECTIONS TO THE DESIGN ===\n* **A FOURTH TERMINATING SITE IS UNROWED**: crn 4 STORE with a non-zero value (`:320-325`).  It is the subject's nearest sibling inside the same `case crm 0:` switch, and under SIBLING PARITY crn 4 is uncovered in BOTH directions -- including the silent-accept read at `:317-318` that the disposition argument leans on.\n* **L, N and C MERGE into one invocation** -- the rule binds only if the words must occupy the same address, and nothing requires that.  Six arms -> four, before the additions.\n* **Arm E is strengthened for free**: `0xEE107612` yields three ZERO fields, so a broken extractor still mostly matches.  **`0xEE79B6B2`** gives `opcode1=3 opcode2=5 crn=9 crm=2 rd=11 l=1` -- six distinct non-trivial fields, turning E into a decode-field anchor at zero cost.\n* **THE NOP WAS NAMED, NOT ENCODED.**  *\"the NOP (`mov r0,r0`)\"* is a LABEL; the word is `0xE1A00000`.  That is precisely this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, recorded four times.\n* **MY NON-INTERFERENCE REASON WAS WRONG AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES.**  I said the tick cannot fire inside a `step` because its input is a `timer_add` callback.  **The tick DOES fire** -- `machine.c:354` initialises `ticks_till_next[n] = 0` and `:727-730` fires at `<= 0`, so every tick function runs on step 1 of every invocation.  What protects the cell is the ELSE branch: `tmr0` is 0 at reset, so the else writes `i80321_isrc` and `tisr` and never `inten`.  *This matters for the free-running anchor arm, which must not assume the tick is inert -- that same step-1 tick calls `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` whenever `!(isrc & inten)`.*\n\n=== VERIFIED SOUND ===  All seven encodings confirmed against BOTH DDI 0406 A8.8.108/A8.8.99 and the emulator's own extractor, field for field, with three near-miss combiner traps cleared (`mia*`, `0xee070f9a`, `0x8afffffa`).  VA 0x8000 verified and **more robust than claimed** -- it holds even under `-Q`, because with the MMU off VA == PA.  *But the margin is ZERO: the L1 table ends at 0x7FFF and the code starts at 0x8000, so any literal pool below the code lands in the table.*  Disposition confirmed as option-set member **4 ACCEPT**, the DC21285 `+0x188` precedent exactly.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED (agy 3.8, scratch clone).  **THE ANCHOR ARM IS THE ONLY THING THAT KILLS M1 AND M5, MEASURED.** ***\n\n    witness, committed HEAD   SCORE 11/26   (15 rows red)\n    witness, with the fix     SCORE 26/26\n    BASE 26/26   RESTORED 26/26   restore verified BOTH parts (cmp CLEAN, binary FRESH)\n\n**THE KILL TABLE VINDICATES THE ADVERSARIAL PASS BY MEASUREMENT, which is the whole point of having built the anchor arm:**\n\n    M1        23/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_M1, ANCHOR_EXC, ANCHOR_CONTRAST\n    M5        25/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_EXC\n    M_OPCODE2 19/26   M_RD15 25/26   M_NREAD 19/26\n\n> **`M1` -- the floor breach -- dies on NOTHING BUT THE THREE ANCHOR ROWS.  `M5` -- the total escape that is not a host kill -- dies on EXACTLY ONE ROW, `ANCHOR_EXC`.**  Every stepping arm is green under both.  *So without the free-running anchor arm the six-arm design would have shipped with a floor breach and a total escape, exactly as the review predicted from reading alone.*\n\nThat is this project's *a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE* rule with both halves present in one day: the review named `M1` and `M5` from the source and said the stepping arms could not see them; the table shows they are seen by three rows and one row respectively, and by nothing else.\n\n`M_OPCODE2` and `M_NREAD` each red the same seven D-arm rows (19/26), and `M_RD15` reds exactly `RD15_SURVIVE` (25/26) -- so the three cheap one-word additions the review prescribed each carry a measured kill rather than being precautionary.\n\n**ORDER: the PRETABLE discriminator HOLDS** -- `PRETABLE.out` at `02:18:05`, `build_BASE.log` at `02:20:51`, so the block was written 2m46s before table 1 and its own mtime proves it.\n\n*** BUT ONE ORDER CLAIM IS NOT EVIDENCED AND I WILL NOT ASSERT IT: whether the COMMITTED witness leg was run BEFORE the edit was applied. ***  `witness_fix.out` carries mtime `02:20:10` and `witness_committed.out` `02:20:23` -- **the fix leg is the EARLIER file** -- and the report does not address ordering anywhere (grep for order/test-first/before-any-edit returns nothing).\n\n**What IS established is weaker but real: the kill table's own `HEAD` leg checks out the committed source, rebuilds and probes, scoring 11/26.**  So a pre-fix measurement exists and is reproducible; what is missing is evidence that it was taken FIRST, which is what witness-ladder clause (i) asks for.  *This project's oracle-correction rule exists because a measurement taken after the edit cannot be distinguished from one fitted to it -- and the two mtimes here are the only thing that speaks to the question, and they point the wrong way.*\n\n**OWED: either a statement from the run that the committed leg preceded the edit, or a re-run of the committed leg from a clean checkout with its mtime recorded.**  Cheap either way, and it is the difference between a witness and a detector in this project's own vocabulary.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROPAGATION CHECK (two-part clearance, RUN not asserted).  **ITS SEC EDIT IS IN NO BUILD TREE, AND ALL THREE BINARIES REPORT `FRESH`.** ***\n\n`cpu_arm_coproc.c` carries this row's uncommitted SEC edit (2 lines removed).  The clearance was run against all three build trees and `est/`:\n\n    tree               part 1 (cmp vs SEC)   part 2 [binary -nt source]\n    build/             DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:48:13)\n    build-asan/        DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:01)\n    /tmp/gxsec-build   DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:42)\n    est/               SOURCE tree -- no binary exists, part 2 N/A (inapplicable, not unrun)\n\n*The other three edited files in the same working tree -- `cpu_ppc_instr.c`, `dev_lca.c`, `dev_sgi_re.c` -- are CLEAN in all three, so this is a single missed propagation rather than a tree-wide drift.*\n\n> *** THE DANGEROUS PART IS THAT PART 2 GIVES NO WARNING: all three binaries are NEWER than their sources, so `FRESH` is TRUE and MEANINGLESS -- the binary is newer than a source that is simply the wrong source. ***  **Part 2 tests staleness against whatever source sits in the tree; only part 1 tests whether that is the source you think it is.**  A round that ran only the freshness half here would measure PRE-FIX ARM coprocessor behaviour and see nothing wrong.\n\n**None of the four files is in the divergence set** (derived, 6 paths), so the clearance genuinely applies and `DIVERGENT` here means UNPROPAGATED rather than by-design.\n\n*** NOT PROPAGATED IN THIS PASS, DELIBERATELY. ***  Propagating without rebuilding is the trap this project has paid for twice, and a build was in flight (agy, `wdcaltdigin` witness).  **Propagate and rebuild together, then re-run BOTH parts** -- recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` with the restore recipe so a fresh session finds it.\n\n*Found by running the clearance at a round boundary rather than at a mutation boundary -- which is the carrier's own finding that the two-part clearance is a ROUND-BOUNDARY obligation and that the sentinel discipline cannot catch this class at all, because nothing mutated anything.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47080:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Evidence: regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt (GPT-6 Astra at max), which enumerated the population properly for the first time.  **This row exists so the next session reads the census instead of re-running it**, which has now happened twice.\n\n*** THE EARLIER FIGURES WERE RIGHT AND THEIR DESCRIPTION WAS WRONG: 370 and 240 count `exit(1)', NOT every `exit()'. ***  Re-derived by removing comments and string literals while preserving line positions, then locating the calls, with an independent character-state lexer returning exactly the same positions:\n\n    src/devices    263 literal `exit('   135 calls after lexical filtering\n    src/cpus       112                    74\n    src/machines    35                    34\n    TOTAL          410                   243   (240 of them exit(1))\n\nLOCATION SPLIT of those 243: 150 runtime handlers and helpers, 80 init/configuration, 13 CPU generators and a standalone experiment.  The debugger is outside that scope and adds three more.\n\nAND BEING INSIDE A RUNTIME FUNCTION IS NOT REACHABILITY.  The 150 split: **98 input-reachable through enabled in-tree wiring**; 33 dead, precluded, or not established reachable; 10 optional instrumentation; 6 unattached or disabled attachments; 2 intentional halt/reboot; 1 host RAM-config check misplaced in an access handler.\n\nOWNERSHIP is an OVERLAY, not a disjoint population -- 22 live exits and 3 others are actively owned (n6 holds eight footbridge sites; alphavmkill one; sgirekills five; lcaisalane and lcabadval eight between them; m88kexit two dead ones).  **A strict open/held filter leaves 83, of which seven belong to in-work LCA repairs, so THE UNOWNED REMAINDER IS 76.**\n\n*** AND ONE OF MY OWN EARLIER QUALIFICATIONS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE, corrected here: lacking a separate CHILD repair id does not mean lacking an open OWNING row.  `sgirekills' explicitly retains its five command exits.  Conversely an incidental mention in census prose is NOT a repair row. ***\n\nSIX E1 PREDICATES were identified -- a one-to-five-line change with an existing test carrier -- and ALL SIX ARE NOW FILED as their own rows: [[mk48nvramkill]], [[mapledmaaddr]], [[xscaleinten]], [[crimewidthkill]], [[vmedefaultkill]], [[fbctrlportkill]].  **This row therefore tracks the ~70 that remain, and they are E2/E3: real rounds, not quick wins.**  The heaviest concentrations are dev_osiop.c (14), cpu_arm_coproc.c (8 of which one is filed), dev_adb.c (7), dev_dreamcast_g2.c (6), dev_pvr.c and dev_igsfb.c (5 each).\n\nTHE ONE CONSTRAINT THAT GOVERNS EVERY DISPOSITION IN THIS BODY, and it is easy to forget: **memory_rw.c turns a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY.**  Returning zero from an ARM or SH device callback gives NO bus-error delivery, so the FAULT member is unavailable there and a round reaching for it owes the exception path first.  CPU translation and instruction handlers have their own direct mechanisms, which is a separate question.\n\n*** THE LARGEST CONCENTRATION HAS BEEN TRIAGED, 2026-09-10: dev_osiop.c (osiopexits/codex_triage_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max; 3 cyber flags, ALL MID-STREAM and non-fatal). ***\n\n**THE FILE HAS SIXTEEN EXPLICIT exit() CALLS, NOT FOURTEEN.**  The census's fourteen are all still executable, and two more exist that it did not list -- **and those two are the ones that turn out to be DEAD**, so the census happened to enumerate exactly the live set without saying why.\n\n    (i)   guest register access or guest-started SCRIPTS      **14**  -- one register default,\n                                                                        thirteen sequencer exits\n    (ii)  unconstructible state or impossible branch          **2**\n    (iii) internal failures where stopping is right           **0**\n\n*** POPULATION (iii) BEING EMPTY IS ITSELF THE FINDING, and it is the one that licenses the whole body of work.  NONE of the sixteen checks establishes corrupted host ownership or any other condition that requires terminating.  The seat says the diagnostic wording -- notably 'cannot be combined' -- does NOT turn these into internal assertions, which is the argument a round would otherwise have to make sixteen times. ***\n\nTHE TWO DEAD PATHS, RECORDED WITH THEIR WARRANTS so nobody re-finds them:\n  * the reserved-phase MOVE default -- an earlier equality check forces the instruction phase to match SOCL; initialisation and every implemented transition produce only handled phases; SET/CLEAR do not touch the phase bits; and a direct SOCL write terminates at the REGISTER DEFAULT before another SCRIPTS execution can run.  *That last clause is why this one is dead only while the register default is still fatal -- fixing the register default could REVIVE it, which a round must not miss.*\n  * the outer instruction-class default is **mathematically unreachable**: the switch evaluates `dcmd & 0xc0' and explicitly handles all four possible results.\n\n**AND IT CORRECTED THE AUDIT'S EFFORT RANKING:** the blanket E3 (`preserve command termination, status and interrupt behaviour') OVERSTATES the obligation for the register default and the redundant table-MOVE equality check, because the real status and completion sequence lives in the STATUS and MSG_IN phases, and the backend-failure branches are ALREADY de-fatalised by earlier rounds and sit outside this census.  So two of the nine prescribed rows are cheaper than the audit implied.\n\n*** THE SEVERITY CEILING, ASKED FOR AND ANSWERED PLAINLY: 'no in-tree runnable workload drives this controller's register/SCRIPTS interface.' ***  All fourteen are guest-reachable in principle and NOTHING IN THIS TREE EXERCISES THEM.  That is worth more than a ranking: it means these rounds cannot reach rung 4, and a witness must plant SCRIPTS by hand.\n\nA TRAP NAMED FOR THE FIRST ROW, and it is the kind that makes a one-line repair wrong: the generic byte path STORES BEFORE DISPATCH, and the word path also stores to DNAD and ADDER before reaching the unhandled-register default -- so restoring only `oldreg' would NOT undo those word stores.  And a blanket backed-accept would enable SOCL writes, **which changes the reserved-phase reachability finding above**.  The two interact.\n\nNINE REPAIR ROWS PRESCRIBED covering the fourteen, in priority order: register default; redundant table-MOVE equality check; MOVE phase mismatch; transfer-control handling; MOVE addressing modes; SELECT validation; WAIT RESELECT; register/ALU instructions; MEMORY MOVE.  Not filed individually yet -- filing nine rows for a controller nothing drives would crowd a queue that already holds live guest-reachable kills on machines this project DOES boot."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47461:   "id": "mapledmaadvance",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47467:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10, split out of [[mapledmaaddr]] so that row can proceed.  **The two are on DIFFERENT SITES and only one of them is that round's.**\n\n`maple_do_dma_xfer()' walks the command list accumulating `addr += datalen_cmd * 4;' per command, and at its tail -- immediately before asserting SYSASIC_EVENT_MAPLE_DMADONE -- does:\n\n        // TODO: Should the DMA address be updated?\n        d->dmaaddr = addr;\n\nSo an advancing cursor IS modelled, and the author recorded that they did not know whether it should be.  **The comment is the finding**: this is not a defect anyone has established, it is an unresolved semantic question sitting in shipped code with no test either way.\n\nWHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: real Maple hardware behaviour for a read of the DMA address register after a completed transfer.  No Dreamcast or Maple documentation is present in `_scratchpad/` -- checked by `ls`, not assumed -- so this needs a source obtained first, on the vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent where a seat's citation was fetched and then VERIFIED LOCALLY rather than trusted.\n\n**TWO EARLY RETURNS LEAVE THE FUNCTION BEFORE THE TAIL** and are part of the same question: the `!d->enable' path and the `addr & 0x1f' alignment refusal both return without touching `d->dmaaddr', so after an aborted transfer the field holds the PROGRAMMED address while after a completed one it holds the advanced address.  Whatever contract is settled has to cover both.\n\n*** DO NOT LET THIS BLOCK [[mapledmaaddr]]. ***  That round deletes a terminating call in the register's READ arm, which is a pure reporter of this field -- it neither creates nor resolves the question above, and the same unverified address is ALREADY handed to the DMA engine on every transfer whether or not a guest ever reads it back.  The dependency runs the other way: whatever is settled here becomes the expected value of that round's disclosed after-DMA row, which is why that row is written to pin the READ ARM'S contract rather than to certify the advanced value.\n\n*** ARITHMETIC CORRECTED 2026-09-10 (mapledmaaddr/codex_readarm_20260910.txt) BEFORE ANYONE BUILT ON IT. ***  This row first described the advance as `addr += datalen_cmd * 4' per command.  **That is one of three terms.**  Each descriptor advances the local cursor by 8 bytes of control words, 4 bytes of command word, and `4 * datalen_cmd' of data, so the committed value is\n\n        C = (P + SUM over descriptors of (12 + 4*D_i))  mod 2^32\n\nTwo consequences for whoever settles the hardware contract: **the sum is modulo 2^32, so \"advanced\" does not imply numerically larger**, and `C != P' is not guaranteed in general -- so a test asserting merely that the value CHANGED is weaker than it looks.\n\n**AND THE EARLY-EXIT DESCRIPTION IN [[mapledmaaddr]] WAS WRONG in a way that matters here:** an early return preserves the field's ENTRY value, which may be a PRIOR transfer's cursor -- it does not restore a programmed address.  The `!d->enable' test also precedes the alignment test, so a disabled and unaligned address never reaches the alignment diagnostic.  Any contract settled for this row has to cover the entry-value case, not just \"programmed\" versus \"advanced\".\n\nAlso relevant to the eventual policy: an alternative length calculation from the CONTROL word sits COMMENTED OUT beside the executable one, so the file records that its author considered a second arithmetic and chose this one."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47998:   "note": "Filed from #528 (mapledmaaddr), whose floor-graded silence row went through seven oracles, each of the first six swept by a diagnostic in a legal shape or a later interval.  The closure is an exact owed transcript computed from the commands (regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py owed()/disasm_line()/noise()/unclean(): the whole session through EOF, every line in full, EOF and exit 0 required).  Census other regress/*_probe.py silence rows and their grades.  Owed by no shipped round.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:48007:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/mapledmaaddr/fable_pass2_20260912.md (verbatim; brief _scratchpad/brief_maple_fable_p2_20260912.md)",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:48008:     "note": "The class named by the flagship adjudication of #528: 'a silence oracle built as a whitelist has, by construction, a shape to hide in'.  Census not yet run."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49444:   "state_note": "*** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  `case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  `case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & (1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and `vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month.  *** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, `IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- which IS the reproduction, since the house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30' with no `device_add' introduced to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd' DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd' alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE REPORTED `alive' FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF instead of waiting out its timeout, so the `spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single `waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB core, reports 'still running', and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample once** -- and `arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its witness will certify the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the same disagreement: my `devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit R10000, so a CORRECT device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 3 + MEMBER 4, AND THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY ARGUMENT. ***  I filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` occurs FIVE times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- with no definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` (`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  **Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the inference that it was cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil literals use a DELETED numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick's `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating them onto `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 16550 breaks on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 7 on `lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP ROW** (implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  *Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *'Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.'*  **The correct statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- `interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The distinction is not pedantic: 'not available' would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4'S WORD 'SEPARATE' IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling it 'separate' is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi's sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the missing test **is the read-kill's entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** -- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d->isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store updates *'a register nothing reads for any purpose'*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`'s READ returns `d->isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive).  The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3's warrant cited a PROM that 'already talks to 0x10018' -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME's *'one bit per site, never shared'* than from LCA's two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write silence the timer-tick diagnostic forever** (grok, minimax, agy).  Disposition 7 holds AS A CENSUS but does NOT pick member 2 over member 3: the `fatal()` already exists, so de-fatalising KEEPS it and latches (grok). **STILL OPEN AND NOT INVENTED:** minimax says Q3 -- what a read at 0x10020 should RETURN post-fix -- **cannot be settled from the packet** and needs an IP30/HEART source, and there is no HEART manual under `_scratchpad/`.  That is an honest UNKNOWN and it outranks a confident guess; the round picks a disposition for Q3 with its reason stated, or defers the read behaviour.  **agy proposes a TICK ROW BY `step 70000`** -- `tickshift` 16 means 65536 cycles -- which would make the tick reachable WITHOUT free-run and **contradicts my 'needs free-run' statement in this row's witness limit.**  Not yet measured; carried as a named question rather than adopted.  *** THE TICK ROW IS BUILDABLE AND IS MEASURED: `step 1' KILLS THE HOST.  The codex/agy split is settled BY MEASUREMENT, and BOTH SEATS WERE HALF RIGHT WHILE THE DECISIVE FACT WAS NAMED BY NEITHER. ***  codex was right that stepping alone is insufficient -- the guard must be ARMED (measured: no store + 70000 steps -> alive; bit 49 + 70000 steps -> alive).  agy was right that free-run is NOT needed, but its stated reason -- a 65536-cycle period implying `step 70000' -- is not why the cheap row works.  **THE MECHANISM, verified by me in the source and not taken from the seat:** `machine_add_tickfunction' sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0' (`machine.c:354') while `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 << tickshift' (`:355'), and `machine_run()' decrements then fires while `<= 0' (`:727').  **So the FIRST `machine_run()' of any session drives it to -1 and fires EVERY tick function immediately** -- `step 1' executes the store and then runs the tick in that same `machine_run()'.  *** A THIRD FACT NEITHER SEAT NAMED, AND IT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: **BIT 50 CANNOT BE SET BY `sw'.** ***  `case 0x10000' does `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a FOUR-BYTE store caps idata below 2^32 -- measured, `sw 0xffffffff' + 70000 steps -> ALIVE.  Arming requires **`sd' = 0xFCE80000** (readback `ld' = 0xDC220000).  **`OP_SW_T0_A3' IS THE ONLY STORE HELPER IN `ip30_abort_probe.py'**, so a tick row copy-pasted from it would arm nothing, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH.  **PERIODICITY IS NOT A STARTUP ARTIFACT** -- the boundary was measured from TWO different warm offsets, both sides: warm=10 gives alive at N=65525 and DEAD at 65526; warm=100 gives alive at 65435 and DEAD at 65436.  Both put tick #2 at `machine_run' #65536 exactly, so ticks fire at MR #1, #65536, #131072.  **A guest arming bit 50 at ANY moment dies within 65536 instructions, with no access in flight.**  *The seat's own first derivation said #65537 and its boundary run measured that false -- it recorded the measurement over its derivation, which is the right way round.*  STABILITY: run 1 and run 2 are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 13 scored rows (9/13, same four FAILs), `IP30TICK_CONTROL=OK` both times.  CONTROLS ALL GREEN, including the two that make the positive attributable: **`tick49 s70k'** (same store, same step count, bit 49 not 50 -> alive) is the discriminator, and **`late s1'** (armed AFTER tick #1 was consumed, one step -> alive) is what proves `tick50 s1' measured THE TICK and not something else about the first instruction.  **L4 APPLIED TO MY OWN PROBE:** `ip30_abort_probe.py`'s `acc imask' row claimed \"a handful of steps cannot reach it\".  MEASURED FALSE.  The row is still safe, for two reasons it did not state (bit 18, and `sw' cannot set bit 50 anyway), and the comment now says so.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND:** codex's constraint is DISCHARGEABLE rather than blocking -- the four-site closure is available because the tick row exists and is measured.  Artifacts at `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/`, all four carrying their completion sentinel, copied off the volatile scratch path the measuring seat itself flagged.  *** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says \"according to Linux/IP30\" three times, so the reference is Linux's `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner's approval on this project's vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d->isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register's store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR IT IN ITS OWN COMMENT: heart.h documents `isr` (0x10030) as READ-ONLY, and the fork implements a WRITE arm -- `/* Clear-on-write (TODO: is this correct?) */  d->isr &= ~idata;`.  The cited document answers that TODO: NO. ***  Clearing goes through `clear_isr` at 0x10028, which this same file already implements, so the model has TWO clear paths where the architecture has ONE, and the fabricated one is the one carrying the self-doubting comment.  IN SCOPE for this round (same case labels, same function, same detector); it is a member 6 BACKED ACCEPT standing WITHOUT a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit.  Disposition deliberately NOT decided here. **HONEST LIMIT, STATED:** a Linux header is a driver author's model, NOT SGI's specification. It is strong evidence about what the registers ARE and about what this emulator MEANT -- because this emulator cites it -- and it is not a hardware contract.  No claim above depends on the difference: declining a read is safe under either reading.  Preserved at regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/HEART_reference_20260917.txt.  *** FABLE 5.1 ADJUDICATION, 2026-09-17.  IDENTITY VERIFIED -- `claude-fable-5-1`, the nonce, AND the verbatim first line of CHECKPOINT.md, so prompt delivery and file access are proven rather than assumed.  Fired LAST, after all 8 scriptable transports and the Opus measurement. *** **SHIP ORDER: (c) commit the reviewed working set -> (b) run `arm_msrmode_probe.py` -> (a) the ip30 fix as its OWN round.**  Serialisation note it supplied and I had not: (b) runs the emulator and (c) runs a gate, and they contend on CPU but NOT on the tree -- so run (b) to completion, THEN (c)'s gate alone. **A2 -- Q3 IS ALREADY ANSWERED AND THE FIX DOES NOT WAIT.**  DECLINE the read at 0x10020; do not mirror `d->isr`, do not return 0-as-a-value.  *** AND IT RULED ON A SECOND DISPOSITION MY FIVE QUESTIONS NEVER REACHED: DECLINE THE 0x10030 **WRITE** ARM TOO. ***  heart.h documents `isr` as read-only and the fork's clear-on-write is a member-6 BACKED ACCEPT standing without a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit -- Linux/IP30 clears via 0x10028, which the file already implements.  Same function, same case labels, same detector family, so same round.  It owes a STATE row (write declined AND `isr` unchanged), not merely an `alive' row. **A3 -- MY FRAMING WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART.**  These are not four defects competing to join a round under the reopening rule.  **They are ONE ABANDONED FEATURE** -- the identical `fatal(\"IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO\"); abort();` at every reachable exit of a rewrite nobody finished.  The unit of work is the FEATURE, not the site, and splitting it would de-fatalise three of four exits and leave the fourth a live host death, which is the de-escalation clause's own hazard.  *** AND IT REFUSED TO LET ME GLOSS THE COST: the 'no new oracle' criterion IS genuinely violated -- the tick needs its own periodicity oracle and its own latch bit, so THE ROUND SHIPS TWO DETECTORS, and the round record must NAME that deviation rather than let a two-probe structure read as an accident. *** **A4 -- IT FOUND MY WEAKEST CORRECTION AND IT IS RIGHT.**  My (iv) said the `acc imask' row is safe *because it writes bit 18 AND because `sw' cannot set bit 50*.  Both clauses are true and **clause (2) MISDIRECTS**: the row's value `1<<18` has no high bits at any width, so width is irrelevant to THIS row -- while 'sw cannot set bit 50' reads as a general safety invariant for imask0, and **the death IS reachable, by `sd 1<<50`**.  *** MY OWN PROBE'S COMMENT IS STRONGER AND MORE HONEST THAN MY SUMMARY OF IT: it calls that row a documented HAZARD and warns that copy-pasting it as a kill row would 'arm NOTHING, stay ALIVE, and score green on a live host death'.  My correction re-labelled a HAZARD as a SAFETY PROPERTY. ***  That is the a-correction-is-not-safer-than-what-it-corrects rule landing on me. **A4(i) -- MY WARRANT OVERCLAIMED TRANSFERABILITY, verified locally:** `dev_sgi_ip32.c` has 6 INTERRUPT_CONNECT/ASSERT sites; **`dev_sgi_ip30.c` has ZERO, and zero `struct interrupt` members.**  'CRIME uses it' proves the IDIOM exists on MIPS; it does not prove member 7 is BUILDABLE for ip30, which has no interrupt topology at all.  The correct statement is *member 7's idiom is available; its application to ip30 requires building the HEART topology and is substantial* -- which is the carrier's own 2026-09-06 lesson (check the member is available on THIS CPU; a precedent real elsewhere is not thereby transferable). **A5 -- THE QUESTION I WAS NOT ASKING, AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE: DOES `ip30abortkill` ACTUALLY OUTRANK `armmsrmodekill`?**  Rung is a FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY orders the queue, and severity rests on real-guest reachability **that is measured for NEITHER**.  Verified locally: `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels today**; only one gate names ip30 at all, and the device's own header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  Meanwhile `armmsrmodekill` kills the PROCESS on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  **So the order between the two HIGHs was set without the measurement that would settle it** -- which is itself the argument for running the arm probe BEFORE the larger ip30 build: the cheap measurement informs the order of the expensive work.  *** THE FIX DIFF IS WRITTEN AND PRESERVED, AND IT FOUND A HOLE IN MY OWN DETECTOR. ***  `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt`.  NOT APPLIED -- the adjudicated ship order puts the ip30 round LAST, after the commit and the arm probe. The diff honours every ruling: TWO latch bits (`IP30_W_TICK` 1<<0, `IP30_W_ISR` 1<<1, 'one bit per site, never shared'), the `(once)` house marker, `break` and never an early `return` so the common tail at :180-183 still runs, the `writeflag` test at 0x10020/0x10028, and on a READ it DECLINES rather than mirroring -- *'odata stays the function's 0 initializer; that is not a modelled value'*.  It also declines the 0x10030 WRITE arm per the Fable ruling, with the comment *'the TODO answers no.  Clearing goes through clear_isr at 0x10028'*.  It carries a control-flow table showing every path lives. *** THE DETECTOR FINDING, AND IT IS FLOOR-RELEVANT: `kill 30 wr` IS SURVIVE-ONLY AND THEREFORE CANNOT SEE THIS REPAIR. ***  Post-fix the host lives on a 0x10030 write whether or not the store is applied, so an `alive` oracle is invariant under the very edit the row exists to pin.  **That row owes a STATE oracle -- write declined AND `isr` unchanged** -- which is the CHANNEL check's vacuous-by-construction shape, knowable now, before any table runs. **AND THE ISR-UNCHANGED ROW MUST COVER BOTH LOADS, with a twist at 0x10028:** `&= ~0` is a no-op today, so a row that only checks the ISR CELL cannot fail there.  Pin **`odata` is not `d->isr`** instead, seeding ISR to a distinctive value first.  Without that, the 0x10028 writeflag repair ships unpinned.  *** THE DETECTOR WAS ATTACKED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED, AND IT HAS CLEAN-SWEEP (d) MUTANTS. Every one is L1 CONSTRUCTION -- pre-ship material still being built -- NOT an L2 breach, because nothing has been committed. ***  That is the whole point of attacking a detector while it is still a draft. **THE MISSES, each 12/12 against the current 12-row probe:** **(1) `M_tick_abort` -- keep the `if`, put `abort()` back.**  MISS.  No session sets imask0 bit 50, so nothing in the probe visits the tick guard at all.  *** THIS IS CLAUSE (b) AT SITE 4 AS WELL AS (d): a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect that the detector cannot see. ***  **(2) `M_tick_bit49`** -- `(1<<50)` -> `(1<<49)`.  MISS: `acc imask` deliberately writes bit 18, and `tick49 s70k` was ALREADY MEASURED alive.  **(3) `M_tick_swap`** -- `imask0` -> `reg_0x20000`, a field the body writes every tick.  MISS: `acc 20000` stores 0x1234 so bit 50 stays clear. **(4) `M_wf20_store_out` -- the `writeflag` wraps only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a LOAD.**  MISS.  **This is the `asicindexcase` (d) shape exactly: the guard is kept and the repair that stops the clobber is nulled** -- the host lives and the guest's load still zeroes the ISR.  *** AND PASS 1 ALREADY ASKED FOR THIS ROW (Q5) AND IT IS STILL NOT THERE -- which is the `#490` lesson repeating: a prescribed row lost between the pass that prescribed it and the list that was written. ***  **(5) `M_wf20_abort_wr` / `M_wf28_abort_wr`** -- `writeflag` present, `abort()` kept INSIDE the write arm.  MISS, because there is no `kill 20 wr` and no `kill 28 wr` row: the probe only ever LOADS at those two offsets. **TWO HONEST NON-BREACHES, and recording them matters as much as the misses:** `M_wf28_store_out` is **EQUIVALENT, not a breach** -- `idata` is 0 on reads (`:87-90`) so `&= ~0` is a no-op, and *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT*.  `M_one_latch_bit` (tick and ISR sharing one `warned`) is **NOT a (d) floor cell**, because the two guards live in DIFFERENT FUNCTIONS and DEPENDS-ON is walked only to the enclosing function boundary, never into callers -- so it is **L3 / contract disclosure, not L2**.  *That bound is what stops this becoming `#392`, and the seat applied it against its own finding.* **ROWS OWED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPS:** a TICK row (arm bit 50 with `sd`, step, expect the announce -- note the existing tick witness is a SEPARATE probe, so either it folds in or the contract must state the round ships TWO detectors); **`kill 20 wr` and `kill 28 wr`**; the **ISR-UNCHANGED** row (seed ISR, load at 0x10020, read back at 0x10030); and a `txt` sibling for the 0x10030 write so `M_latch_silent_30` cannot delete the diagnostic silently. **AND ONE FORWARD-LOOKING MUTANT WORTH THE CONTRACT:** `M_wf20_len8` -- `writeflag == MEM_WRITE && len == 8` -- misses today only because there is no write row at 0x10020, and **HEART registers are `u64`, so it becomes real the moment a write row is added using `sw`**.  That is the `sd`/`sw` trap from the tick measurement arriving in the detector.  *** THE OWED ROWS ARE WRITTEN (L1 CONSTRUCTION, NOT YET RUN). ***  Five mutants that scored 12/12 against the probe are now closed by construction: **`tick 50`** (closes M_tick_abort -- which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see -- and M_tick_swap); **`tick 49`**, its DISCRIMINATOR, so `tick 50` cannot pass for an unrelated reason and M_tick_bit49 cannot walk free; **`kill 20 wr` / `kill 28 wr`**, because the probe only ever LOADED at those offsets so an abort() kept inside the WRITE arm was invisible; **`isr keep`**, the `asicindexcase` row that closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a load, which leaves the host ALIVE and the ISR CLOBBERED, worse than the defect being fixed; and **`kill 30 txt`**, a diagnosis sibling for the 0x10030 write arm so M_latch_silent_30 cannot delete its announce silently. *** THE TICK ROW REQUIRED A NEW OPCODE, AND THAT IS THE TRAP, NOT A DETAIL: the probe had ONLY `sw`, and a 4-byte store CANNOT SET imask0 BIT 50 because `case 0x10000` reads through `memory_readmax64`.  A tick row written with the existing helper would arm NOTHING, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***  Added `OP_SD_T0_A3` (0xFCE80000) and `OP_LD_V0_AT` (0xDC220000), and **DECODED BOTH FROM OUTSIDE THE CODE before trusting them** -- opcode 63 = sd with base $7/rt $8, opcode 55 = ld with base $1/rt $2 -- because this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap is that a wrong constant is invisible when the probe reads the same constant. **`isr keep` READS `died` PRE-FIX AND THAT IS CORRECT**: its seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts on the committed build.  That is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure, and the row comment says so. **NOT YET RUN** -- five grok seats are loading the machine and this probe's settle/spin are LOAD-SENSITIVE oracles.  The new rows are CONSTRUCTED, not MEASURED, and the pre-fix run owes a re-run with this instrument (a comparison owes ONE instrument).  *** L4 -- SIX PASS-1 SEAT CELLS CARRIED ONE STAMPED NOTE, CORRECTED 2026-09-17 FROM EACH SEAT'S OWN FILE. ***  `precommit_check.sh` section O caught it (`BOILERPLATE_FAIL`, `ip30abortkill x6`), and the defect is not untidiness.  **The six seats named FIVE different weakest dispositions** -- agy #6/#4, grok its own reframing, glm **#1**, kimi **#4**, deepseek **#5**, minimax **#6** -- **and split THREE WAYS on Q3**: agy/grok/kimi say the `0x10020` read returns **0**, deepseek says it returns **`d->isr`**, and minimax says it is **not settleable from the packet without the HEART spec**.  *A stamped note renders that as unanimity* -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape reached by a different road, since every seat here really did answer. **AND THE ERASED DISSENT WAS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE.**  minimax's refusal to guess on Q3 is what made fetching an IP30/HEART reference a decision for the owner instead of letting three seats' confident `0` stand as consensus; and grok's Q6 -- *\"that is not coverage debt, that is the defect left live\"* -- contradicts three seats who said the tick row may be filed. *The `ev` fields were per-seat and correct throughout, so the answers were never lost; only the notes were stamped.  That is why the repair was a read rather than a recovery -- and it is the argument for section O existing at all, because nothing else in the harness compares one seat's cell against another's.*  *** THE 15-ROW INSTRUMENT'S PRE-FIX BASELINE WAS RUN 2026-09-17, AND IT CAUGHT A ROW THAT WAS RED ON A CORRECT BUILD.  L1 construction repair, in-round. ***  **FIRST, THE COUNT WAS WRONG IN MY OWN NOTE:** the probe emits **18** rows, not the 15 a `grep -c '^\\s*row('` reported -- the probe's own `IP30ABORT_RESULT` line is the authority, and the grep was another correlate.  Baseline: **`IP30ABORT_RESULT=10/18`**, sentinel `IP30ABORT_DONE`, preserved at `probe_HEAD_preaccfix_20260917.out`. **SEVEN OF THE EIGHT FAILURES ARE THE DEFECT REPRODUCING**, at both directions of all three offsets plus the tick and the ISR clobber: `kill 20 rd`, `kill 28 rd`, `kill 30 wr`, `kill 20 wr`, `kill 28 wr`, `tick 50`, `isr keep`.  **Every control is GREEN** -- `live` returns `0x11223344`, the accept rows are serviced, and *** `tick 49` STAYS ALIVE WHERE `tick 50` DIES ***, which is the discriminating pair proving it is bit 50 rather than the tick path generally. *** THE EIGHTH FAILURE WAS NOT THE DEFECT AND NOT A SECOND ONE: `acc 20000` EXPECTED A COUNTER NOT TO COUNT. ***  It stored `0x1234` and wanted it back; it read `0x161c`.  **`0x161c - 0x1234 = 0x3e8 = 1000` EXACTLY**, which is `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000` in `DEVICE_TICK` -- one tick between the store and the load.  *The DEVICE was right and the ROW was wrong.*  The access arm alone reads like a plain r/w register; only the file's own `/*  A counter  */` comment and the tick body say otherwise. **AS WRITTEN IT MANUFACTURED A PHANTOM FAILURE ON A CORRECT BUILD, so it could never signal the defect this probe exists for** -- the vacuity class inside the round's own witness, and the reason it is L1 (pre-ship construction) rather than filed. **THE REPLACEMENT ORACLE IS STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES.**  The counter is always a multiple of 1000 (it starts at 0 and only ever `+= 1000`) and `0x1234` = 4660 is NOT, so `readback - 0x1234` is an exact multiple of 1000 **iff the store landed**, and is never one if the store was lost.  The row now pins THAT THE STORE REACHED `reg_0x20000` -- which the old literal compare only did by accident on a tickless run -- while tolerating any number of ticks. *Found by doing the test-first step the round already owed: re-running the pre-fix baseline on the CURRENT instrument, because the last recorded figure (6/9) came from a retired nine-row one.  That is the same two-instrument defect found in `sgirergbroundtrip` (4/6 vs 10/10) an hour earlier, caught here BEFORE it became a record rather than after.*  *** FIXED AND MEASURED 2026-09-17 AS `#531`.  PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18 [18-ROW INSTRUMENT, SUPERSEDED BY 8/21 -> 21/21 -- see the correction at the end of this note], with the re-pin's own red measured separately at 8/18 [the order proof, which STAYS on the 18-row form by design]. ***  All four guest-reachable `abort()` calls de-fatalised; live `abort()` 4 -> 0, `return` statements 6 -> 6 (no early return, the common tail still runs). **The disposition was settled by `heart.h`, the header this file cites three times**: `set_isr`/`clear_isr` are write-side (member 4 on WRITE, member 3 on READ) and `isr` is READ-ONLY, so `0x10030`'s clear-on-write is dropped -- *the file's own \"TODO: is this correct?\" answered NO by its own source*. **Two L1 row defects the fix exposed, both repaired in-round:** `acc 20000` expected a counter not to count (`0x161c - 0x1234 = 1000` exactly, the tick stride) and was RED ON A CORRECT BUILD; `isr keep` read the FIRST of three loads instead of the last, invisible pre-fix because its seeding write aborted. **OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2 on the real diff.**  *** PASS 2 DID NOT RATIFY (2026-09-17, panel _scratchpad/panel_20260917_215210).  SEVEN scriptable seats answered; the kimi CLI was on its weekly 403 wall and is COVERED by its kimi-k3:cloud substitute, not lost.  opus5 STILL RUNNING; fable5 HELD by standing directive until every other seat has spoken.  So this stage stands at 7 of 9 and any ruling drawn from it is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  FOUR findings, each verified by me against the code rather than taken on the seat's word.  (L4) the latch comment's 'ONE BIT PER SITE' is false -- five ISR paths share one bit -- REPAIRED to state the real invariant, that latch granularity must match the marker's qualification: both messages end in an unqualified '(once)', which claims one emission per device instance, so one bit apiece is what makes the marker true.  (L1) the tick pair stopped discriminating post-fix, REPAIRED by re-aiming both rows onto the announce channel and adding 'tick 50 txt'.  (L1) 'kill 30 txt' was bound to the 'isr keep' transcript and read the 0x10020 announce -- vacuous for its own named mutant; STAGED.  (L2 FLOOR BREACH) nothing observed that d->isr &= ~idata still runs at 0x10028, a clause-(d) clean sweep four seats reached independently; STAGED as row 'isr clr', with 'isr drop 30' beside it for the re-inserted clear at 0x10030.  The three staged repairs were deliberately NOT applied while the 19-row measurement was executing -- a comparison run owes ONE instrument.  Round figures will be cited from a single 21-row instrument; the 18-row form is frozen at ip30_abort_probe.18row_20260917.frozen.py so the opus table stays attributable.  *** THESE REPAIRS OWE THEIR OWN PASS 2, per the repair-chain rule. ***  *** 21-ROW FIGURES MEASURED 2026-09-17: 8/21 pre-fix, 21/21 post-fix, on ONE instrument. The 18-row pair (11/18, 18/18) is SUPERSEDED rather than stale -- both were correct measurements of an instrument that then grew by three repairs and two floor rows, and citing a pre-fix number from one instrument beside a post-fix number from another is exactly what the one-instrument rule refuses. ***  *** THE FIGURES IN THIS NOTE ARE SUPERSEDED AND ARE CORRECTED HERE RATHER THAN REWRITTEN, so the record shows what was claimed and when.  `PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18' was a correct measurement of the 18-ROW instrument, taken before pass 2.  Pass 2 then found four defects; repairing them added `tick 50 txt', re-bound `kill 30 txt', and added the two floor rows `isr clr' and `isr drop 30', growing the instrument to 21 rows.  THE ROUND'S FIGURES ARE 8/21 PRE-FIX (PREFIX_21row_20260917.out) AND 21/21 POST-FIX (probe_FIX531_21row_20260917.out), both on that one instrument.  Note also that this note's `OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2' line is DISCHARGED -- the pass ran, did not ratify, and its findings are recorded in the review entries on this row. ***  *** THE opus5 PASS-2 SEAT LANDED AND DID NOT RATIFY: SIX CLEAN SWEEPS AT 19/19. ***  The stage is now 8 of 9; fable5 remains held by standing directive.  The fix is APPROVED UNCHANGED by that seat; the DETECTOR owes a CLASS CLOSURE under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes have now found breaches of one class (announce ATTRIBUTION), so the next repair enumerates the whole space from the function's own inputs and closes every cell in ONE diff rather than cell by cell.  THE CLASS: six announce sites, of which only a SINGLE-ACCESS session can attribute one, because all five ISR sites print one identical string behind one shared bit.  *** KILL TABLE 1: THE FLOOR IS DISCHARGED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY 21/21. *** Six mutants in a scratch clone; baseline NAMED (the round's post-fix working set, not HEAD), cmp'd SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in the clone with [binary -nt source] as its own freshness proof; clone-local sentinel; restore from the named baseline, never a script backup; restore verified cmp SAME against SEC and the sentinel cleared only after the rebuild was fresh.  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE.  base 21/21 | M1_del28store 20/21 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050) | M2_reins30clear 20/21 (isr drop 30, 0x484a->0x5a5a) | M3_tickquiet 20/21 (tick 50 txt) | M4_quiet30 20/21 (kill 30 txt) | M5_bit49 19/21 (tick 50 txt AND tick 49, in OPPOSITE directions) | M6_sharedlatch 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP, ESCAPED.  EVERY REPAIR MADE IN RESPONSE TO A SEAT IS NOW MEASURED FAILABLE: M1 is the opus5 seat's severest escape (19/19 pre-repair) and the row four packet-fed seats converged on kills it; M4 is agy's cell, 19/19 on the old binding and dead on the re-bound row; M2's 0x484a matches the arithmetic derived BEFORE the run.  M5's signature is not a red but a MOVE -- the announce leaves bit 50 and appears at bit 49, which only that mutant produces.  *** M6 ESCAPED AND WAS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFORE THE RUN: PRETABLE.out (mtime 22:58:19, against the table's 22:59:36) names it, derives it -- every session is a fresh process, so no row exercises both announce classes in one device lifetime -- and discloses it.  The pre-table block asked its first mandated application to CHECK the CHANNEL rule rather than obey it; this is the first test of 'a row's channel under its mutant is knowable at WRITING time' in this project, and it HELD. ***  CLASS CLOSURE APPLIED (21 -> 27 rows) under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes found breaches of ONE class, so the whole announce-attribution space is enumerated from the function's own inputs and closed in ONE diff: the two unattributed WRITE announces, the latch COLLISION, the latch DEFEAT, and the declined read's VALUE.  Two oracle shapes are new to this probe: `latch 1x' COUNTS announces (a defeated latch reads `yes' to a presence oracle either way) and `xbit' puts BOTH announce classes in ONE device lifetime, which no fresh-process row can do.  KILL TABLE 2 IS RUNNING to VERIFY THE ENUMERATION, with PRETABLE2.out written first (23:44:37 against 23:44:43).  *** KILL TABLE 2: THE CLASS CLOSURE VERIFIED, ZERO NEW BREACHES -- the #499 stop condition MET, with its denominator stated. ***  base 27/27 | M6_sharedlatch 26/27 (xbit isr, 0->1) | M7_silent20wr 25/27 (kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr) | M8_silent28wr 26/27 (kill 28 wr txt) | M9_nolatch 26/27 (latch 1x, 2->1) | M10_mirror20 26/27 (isr 20 rd val, 0x5a5a->0x0) | M1_del28store 26/27 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, binary fresh, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  *** M6 IS THE POINT: 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP BEFORE, 26/27 AFTER. ***  DENOMINATOR: six announce sites, two latch failure modes, one declined-read value.  The two axes NOT swept -- width/len (#499's own axis) and position/translation (clause (f)) -- are DISCLOSED in the probe's contract, not left absent.  *** M9 PROVES THE COUNT ORACLE WAS NECESSARY RATHER THAN DECORATIVE: latch 1x got=2 want=1.  A PRESENCE oracle reads `yes' on the fix AND on the mutant and cannot see the defect at all -- the CHANNEL vacuity arriving one level along, inside the repair for it. ***  M1 WAS RE-RUN ON PURPOSE: #462 measured that adding rows perturbs other mutants' scores, and PRETABLE2.out stated plainly that `six new independent sessions cannot perturb it' was REASONED NOT MEASURED, naming M1 as its single test.  Identical to table 1 -- the claim is now measured.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE, WITH A COST: M6 reds xbit isr ALONE; M7 reds kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr, because the xbit session's ISR access is itself a 0x10020 write.  So xbit isr is NOT a pure collision detector, and only kill 20 wr txt separates the two causes -- the two rows are COUPLED, and the probe header now says so.  CONTRACT (clause (e)) WRITTEN INTO THE PROBE HEADER, where the floor requires it, together with the ATTRIBUTION RULE (a row can attribute an announce only from a SINGLE-ACCESS session) so a future row cannot repeat the kill-30-txt defect by accident.  The header's own stale claim -- `a WITNESS attempt, NOT a shipped detector' -- was corrected in place.  *** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: '5. `isr 20 rd' / `isr 28 rd' -- load does not clobber ISR; load's VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don't-mirror)'.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd val' and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the same pattern: grok's design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER'S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is 'a row list rewritten from a panel's findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block's own prescribed rows, not merely extended with the new ones.'  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in a SEAT'S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row list against that.  So the #490 rule was obeyed and the row was still lost: the diff ran against the wrong source document.  AND THE PRETABLE MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN NEUTRAL: PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as 'decline, no value to pin', so the one artifact #490 DOES mandate diffing against positively asserted that neither row was owed.  *A pre-table block that mis-classifies a cell does not merely fail to catch the omission; it CERTIFIES it.*  grok's correction is one sentence: A DECLINE HAS A VALUE, AND IT IS 0.  PROPOSED REMEDY, FILED NOT BUILT (a harness change owing two negative controls): a round whose fix design was reviewed by a seat DIFFS ITS FINAL ROW LIST AGAINST THAT SEAT'S PRESCRIBED ROWS, as #490 mandates for the PRETABLE -- and the PRETABLE should CARRY those prescriptions forward so the two documents cannot disagree silently.  Cheapest mechanical form is existence-only: if a FIXDIFF_<seat>_*.txt sits beside the killtable dir, each of its numbered prescriptions must map to a shipped row or an explicit no-row sentence.  *** KILL TABLE 3: THE SIBLING-PARITY REPAIR, MEASURED. ***  base 28/28 | M11_mirror28 27/28 (`isr 28 rd val', 0x5a5a -> 0x0).  Sentinels verified; restore cmp SAME against SEC; clone removed.  PRETABLE3.out written 00:49:41 against the table's 00:50:16.  *** PRETABLE.out AND PRETABLE2.out WERE DELIBERATELY NOT EDITED TO CARRY THE CORRECTION: their mtimes ARE the order proofs for tables 1 and 2, and editing either would reset the mtime and DESTROY the property the block exists to provide.  A correction to an order-proof artifact goes in the NEXT block. ***  The contract in the probe header now names BOTH read arms for the mirroring claim; it previously named only 0x10020, which was an overclaim of exactly the kind clause (e) is meant to prevent.  *** THE §F2 ADJUDICATION PASS RAN ON 8 OF 9 (seven scriptable seats + the opus5 seat outstanding at the time of writing); FABLE REMAINS HELD BY STANDING DIRECTIVE AND SPEAKS LAST.  Any ruling here is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  It found ONE floor breach (`isr 28 rd val'), which is repaired and measured, and TWO wrong warrants of mine, both corrected.  The F13 split (2 REFINE / 1 UPHOLD) is RECORDED, NOT RESOLVED -- the stopping rule files it, and a disagreement must not be settled by whoever writes it up.  *** THE opus5 §F2 SEAT: EIGHT MUTANTS, ALL 27/27 -- TOTAL CLEAN SWEEPS OF THE SHIPPED INSTRUMENT -- AND SIX LIE INSIDE CELLS PRETABLE2's OWN ENUMERATION DECLARES CLOSED. ***  Scratch clone, baseline NAMED (md5 0f012a03175541c085d90331ae395abe) and cmp SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in-clone with -nt verified, clone-local sentinel, instrument FROZEN (probe.frozen.py md5 ca309b97085608e9fb4eb0ff0be08095) for the whole comparison, every mutant dry-run-diffed to exactly one line, MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND a hard fail.  Every log ends IP30ABORT_DONE with IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK.  Artifacts PRESERVED BY THE MAIN LOOP to regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opusF2_20260918/ because the seat is read-only and said so rather than writing into the repo.  THE EIGHT: MA_assign20wr (|= -> =, 0x10020-WR latch set, ONE CHARACTER) | MD_assigntick (same at TICK) | MB_nolatchset20wr | MF_nolatchset28rd | MG_nolatchset30wr | ME_nolatchsettick (all: delete the latch SET) | MH_nocounter (delete d->reg_0x20000 += 1000) | MC_len4 (&& len == 4 on the 0x10020 write guard).  *** IT OVERTURNED ITS OWN F17 AS UNDER-STATED, WHICH IS THE FINDING I WAS LEAST LIKELY TO REACH: 'THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE.  IT IS SIX DUPLICATED STATEMENTS, ONE BESIDE EVERY ANNOUNCE -- the same duplication the announce axis was decomposed for.' ***  The real space is {6 sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} = twelve cells, plus {2 collision directions} = FOURTEEN.  The closure rowed SIX.  In its own words: 'my finding bought you a repair for the two instances I happened to build, and the packet closed the CELLS rather than the CLASS -- which is the #499 shape, and I handed you the material for it.'  WHY THE TWO NEW ROWS CLOSE LESS THAN THEY APPEAR TO, and both reasons are structural:  (a) `latch 1x' closes exactly ONE cell -- it visits 0x10020-RD then 0x10028-RD, and the FIRST access latches, so the SECOND arm's latch-set is never load-bearing.  MF_nolatchset28rd deletes the latch set in an arm that row literally traverses: 27/27.  (b) `xbit isr'/`xbit tick' closes NEITHER collision direction.  Its session runs TICK then ISR -- the OPPOSITE order from the hazard the product comment names ('a guest write to Set-ISR would silence the TIMER diagnostic forever').  Seeing that needs tick -> ISR -> tick.  MA and MD both 27/27.  *** THAT IS A CLAIM -> ROW FAILURE ON THE ROUND'S OWN LOAD-BEARING PRODUCT COMMENT, and the pre-table CHANNEL check should have caught it at WRITING time. ***  The seat noted the gap is not carelessness: the probe SINGLE-STEPS, and a second tick costs ~65536 steps under machine.c's ticks_till_next arithmetic, so the cell is open because the probe's SHAPE made it expensive.  A free-running breakpoint-and-continue session (the sh_intprio_probe.py shape) gets it cheaply.  *** AND F1's INVARIANT HAS NO ROW PINNING ITS OBSERVABLE HALF: ISRMSG/TICKMSG stop at '...not implemented' and EXCLUDE `(once)', so deleting the suppression marker from all six strings passes all 27 rows. ***  The round's central design claim -- latch granularity matches the marker's qualification -- is unobserved on the marker side.  Derived by reading, not measured.  F6: *** MEASURED FALSE AND CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `acc 20000' accepts (readback - 0x1234) % 1000 == 0 and N = 0 IS ADMISSIBLE, so with the increment deleted the readback is exactly 0x1234, residue 0, row GREEN (MH_nocounter 27/27).  Re-derived here independently before the correction.  The comment had been wrong TWICE -- first crediting `tick 49', then my correction crediting `acc 20000' -- and the CHANGELOG's 'strictly stronger' was also false: stronger for STORE REACHABILITY, weaker for COUNTER LIVENESS.  Both corrected; the real state is an L3, the de-escalation ACCEPT side of the de-fatalised TICK arm, unpinned.  D2 ANSWERED, which no other seat could: asked to name a regression the re-pinned oracle was pinned PAST, it named THE DELETION OF THE COUNTER INCREMENT and measured it.  THE TWO DISCLOSED AXES, RULED ON: WIDTH/len is NOT floor by the pcc2widthkills bound (the body never tests len) -- but it is now a MEASURED escape (MC_len4 27/27) and the seat notes the reason clause (e) excuses value-domains is ABSENT here: len takes at most four values and the probe already has guest_store64/guest_load64.  It also observed that d->isr is uint64_t while EVERY value the probe drives is <= 16 bits, so `d->isr &= ~(uint32_t)idata' -- a plausible C slip wiping the top 32 bits on every clear_isr -- is green by construction.  POSITION/TRANSLATION: the seat says my disclosure UNDERSTATES coverage, because isr keep / isr clr / isr drop 30 all write at 0x10020/0x10028 and read back at 0x10030, which IS clause (f)'s prescribed anchor (a second access path to the same cell).  What remains unanchored is cross-FIELD aliasing.  SEVERITY RANKING IT SUPPLIED, so the repair is scoped rather than chased: (1) ME_nolatchsettick is the worst -- one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50 yields AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK, FOREVER, which is floodclass, guest-drivable, on the arm this round de-fatalised; (2) MH_nocounter is the L3; (3) MA/MD falsify the two-bit design's stated reason; (4) MB/MF/MG are bounded per-arm floods.  *** THE CHAIN DOES NOT CLOSE, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR STATED: diff delivered and read in full; roster INCOMPLETE (fable5 held at the time); swept 4 latch-set sites x delete, 2 collision directions x |= -> =, 1 width conjunct, 1 tick accept-side = EIGHT mutant shapes across FOUR axes, EIGHT breaches, ZERO clean. ***  RECORDS ITEMS IT RAISED: (a) the witness pair 8/21 -> 21/21 is cited from an instrument that no longer ships -- the detector is 28 rows and there is no pre-fix run at that width; it warns explicitly DO NOT DERIVE 8/27 ON PAPER, which is the frozen-number class.  The main loop had recorded this as OWED in OUTSTANDING_BUGS before the seat landed, independently.  (b) ip30setisrassign in ledger.json and not OUTSTANDING_BUGS -- ALREADY DISCHARGED: the main loop entered it after the seat began, the same time-indexing effect the #507 case showed.  AND IT RECORDED AN APPARATUS FAILURE OF ITS OWN, unprompted: its first table-2 mutator was written through a bash heredoc and lost a backslash level in each direction, turning a Python string literal into a syntax error.  The table aborted at mutant 1 with the tree still cmp SAME -- the carrier's documented hazard, and the hard-fail-on-text-not-found rule is what stopped it mis-mutating quietly.  *The main loop hit the identical trap on the same file the same night and fixed it the same way, with chr(10).*  *** THE fable5 ADJUDICATION -- NINTH AND LAST SEAT, ROSTER NOW 9/9, THE §F2 RULING IS NO LONGER PROVISIONAL. ***  SEAT VERIFIED BY EVIDENCE, NOT ASSUMED, per the owner's standing requirement: it returned the liveness token FABLE_ADJ_ALIVE_20260918, the VERBATIM first line of CHECKPOINT.md ('# CHECKPOINT -- 2026-09-18 ~00:4x', which matches what the main loop wrote), and NAMED ITS OWN MODEL -- Claude Fable 5.1 (claude-fable-5-1).  It also stated it had verified the tree under review was GXEMUL-SEC/src/ rather than a 2021 copy before ruling.  *** VERDICT: RATIFY THE FIX; DO NOT RATIFY THE DETECTOR AS SHIPPED.  THE LATCH-AXIS CLOSURE IS IN-ROUND, NOT FILED. ***  Q1 WARRANT, and it is the part that settles a genuine rule collision the main loop refused to resolve by writing it up: SIX of opus5's eight escapes (MA, MB, MD, ME, MF, MG) are clause-(d) mutants of predicates THE FIX'S OWN DIFF INTRODUCED -- the `d->warned |= IP30_W_*' sets -- which is the canonical 'keep the guard, null its repair' shape, and the vacuity floor makes that L2: replace IN-ROUND however late it is found.  Independently, the shipped CONTRACT claimed to catch 'collapsing the two latch bits' and 'defeating the latch mask outright', both MEASURED FALSE -- an L4 that must be edited in-round anyway, and once the contract is being corrected and the cells are buildable the floor demands rows rather than a file-the-rest note.  Filing was REFUSED specifically because this closure was mis-declared complete once already.  ONE HONEST CARVE-OUT it allowed: a cell genuinely UNBUILDABLE with this fresh-process apparatus may be disclosed with its apparatus reason -- but only after being MEASURED unbuildable.  The single candidate is ME (the flood cell), which needs TWO forced DEVICE_TICKs.  *** Q2: IT REFUTED THE MAIN LOOP'S OWN CORRECTION, AND THE REFUTATION WAS VERIFIED HERE IN ONE LOOK. ***  The main loop had graded the unpinned counter an L3 DE-ESCALATION OBLIGATION.  DEVICE_TICK reads `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000;' UNCONDITIONALLY, two lines ABOVE the `if (d->imask0 & ((int64_t)1<<50))' guard the fix de-fatalised.  The counter runs on EVERY tick regardless of bit 50, so MH_nocounter mutates PRE-EXISTING, FIX-INDEPENDENT behaviour: COVERAGE DEBT, filed as optional, NOT a clause-(d)/L3 obligation.  And the accept side of the predicate that WAS de-fatalised is pinned, by `tick 50' plus `tick 50 txt' -- so the round's accept-side claim is TRUE and the main loop's correction impugned it wrongly.  *** THE LINEAGE IS THE LESSON: `tick 49' -> `acc 20000' -> 'L3 obligation'.  THREE CLAIMS, TWO OF THEM CORRECTIONS, AND ONLY THE L4 DELETION WAS RIGHT.  In its words: 'the correction inherited more authority than the fact, and the over-grade then propagated into the CHANGELOG.' ***  CORRECTED IN BOTH PLACES.  Q3: UPHELD opus5 -- F13, not F10, was the disposition to least trust, and the main loop's §OWED misfiled its own doubt.  Verified against the diff: the read (else) arms fired `d->warned |= IP30_W_ISR' plus the shared write-side message, so a read DID NOT GET an announce -- it CONSUMED the shared bit for all five paths, and by P3's byte-identity a single BENIGN guest READ of 0x10020 permanently silenced the genuine write-side diagnostic.  *** AND IT NAMED THE INCOHERENT CELL: axes {read announces Y/N} x {message per-offset-distinct Y/N}; rejecting F10 while keeping F13 sits at (announce Y, distinct N) -- an undistinguished FALSE message that steals the write-side bit, defensible on neither axis. ***  RULING: adopt MEMBER 2, SILENT DECLINE, for both read arms.  APPLIED, and it SHRANK the closure: ISR announce sites 5 -> 3, so the space is {4 sites} x {announce, latch} + 2 directions = TEN cells, not fourteen.  *Resolving the design question removed four cells of detector work rather than adding them.*  Q4: CONVERGING, NOT #392 -- the FIX has been stable and approved since pass 1 and every pass has been about the DETECTOR -- *** BUT CLOSURE WAS DECLARED ONE PASS EARLY AND THAT DECLARATION IS FALSIFIED. ***  Kill table 2's enumeration counted the latch mechanism as TWO cells when it is roughly eight, so 'ZERO NEW BREACHES / the closing pass VERIFIED THE ENUMERATION' is repair-chain Correction 1 exactly: A BARE ZERO AGAINST A WRONG ENUMERATION DOES NOT CLOSE A CHAIN -- and this round supplied the counter-example to its own citation of that rule.  CORRECTED IN PLACE.  *** THE PRE-COMMITTED FORK THAT MAKES IT TERMINATE: one closure diff, one verifying pass whose job is to confirm ZERO cells outside the stated enumeration with its denominator named.  If that pass finds only already-enumerated cells now killed -> THE CHAIN CLOSES.  If a fifth mutant escapes OUTSIDE the enumeration -> the fresh-process-probe-versus-shared-lifetime-latch ARCHITECTURE is the root cause, and the latch axis drops WHOLESALE to disclosed debt rather than being chased into pass six. ***  D1 UPHELD (TWO bits, one per distinct MESSAGE).  D2 PARTLY UPHELD, and it answered glm's challenge concretely: the regression the re-pinned oracle was pinned PAST is THE ENTIRE LATCH AXIS -- the green instrument was blind to MA through MG.  WHAT ALL NINE MISSED, in its words: nobody enumerated the latch failure modes PER-SITE, PER-DIRECTION until opus5, and P3's byte-identity -- cited as correct design AND as the thing that hid F3 -- is also what made the read-arm announce a false, bit-stealing diagnostic.  *** COMMIT IS HELD on five obligations.  FOUR ARE DONE: the counter regrade, the contract correction, the stop-condition correction, and the F13 silent decline.  THE FIFTH -- the latch-axis closure rows -- REMAINS.  The fix itself ships unchanged. ***  *** KILL TABLE 4 -- THE VERIFYING PASS fable5 PRE-COMMITTED.  SIX OF EIGHT SCORED AS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFOREHAND, AND BOTH MISSES WERE MINE. ***  base 32/32 | V1_assign20wr 31/32 (xbit rev) | V3_nolatch20wr 31/32 (latch 1x) | V4_nolatch28wr 31/32 (latch 28wr) | V5_nolatch30wr 31/32 (latch 30wr) | V6_nolatchtick 30/32 (latch tick AND xbit rev -- killed by TWO rows where one was predicted) | V7_nocounter 32/32 ESCAPED AS PREDICTED | V2_assigntick 32/32 ESCAPED (predicted KILL) | V8_len4 28/32 KILLED FOUR ROWS (predicted ESCAPE).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  PRETABLE4.out 03:06:49 against the table's 03:06:58.  *** THE TWO MISSES ARE DIFFERENT KINDS OF WRONG AND CONFLATING THEM WOULD HAVE HIDDEN ONE. ***  (A) V2 -- A ROW I BUILT WRONG.  A clobber can only bite if the clobbered class's bit was ALREADY SET.  `xbit isr' runs TICK -> ISR, so when the tick assigns, the ISR bit has never been set, nothing is lost, and the row is GREEN on the mutant (derived: warned=0x0 -> tick assigns -> warned=0x1; !(0x1 & 0x2) is TRUE, so the ISR still announces, count 1).  I gave the mirror the opposite ORDER but not the opposite OPERAND-AND-COUNT.  *** THE #499 SHAPE A THIRD TIME THIS ROUND, AND IT IS THE SAME DEFECT THE opus5 SEAT DIAGNOSED IN THE ORIGINAL `xbit', REPRODUCED INSIDE THE REPAIR FOR IT. ***  REPAIRED: `xbit rev2' (ISR -> TICK -> ISR, counting ISRMSG), ADDED rather than re-aimed because `xbit isr' still carries a real claim.  (B) V8 -- A MUTANT I BUILT WRONG, AND THIS IS THE MORE DANGEROUS FAILURE BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE A SUCCESS.  It was anchored on the FIRST two-tab `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)' in the file, which is at :172 inside `case 0x10000' -- THE INTERRUPT MASK REGISTER, not 0x10020.  Every tick row arms bit 50 with a 64-bit `sd' (len 8), so `&& len == 4' REFUSED that store, bit 50 was never set, no tick announced, and four tick rows reddened FOR A REASON UNRELATED TO THE CELL UNDER TEST.  *** HAD I NOT CHECKED **WHICH** ROWS DIED I WOULD HAVE RECORDED THE WIDTH AXIS AS COVERED WHEN IT WAS NEVER TESTED. ***  A mutant that dies for the wrong reason misleads exactly as much as one that will not die; the carrier's three explanations apply and this is case 1, MIS-BUILT.  REPAIRED: anchored on guard #3 (:202, case 0x10020), verified by a dry-run diff that shows it landing in the right arm.  *** AND THE MIS-BUILD LEFT A REAL FINDING, KEPT RATHER THAN DISCARDED: THE DETECTOR DOES CATCH A WIDTH NARROWING AT imask0, WITH A FOUR-ROW SIGNATURE -- coverage on an arm this round never enumerated.  The tick rows collectively pin that imask0 accepts a 64-bit write.  Not a floor cell (the fix does not touch imask0), but real, and found by accident. ***  V7 ESCAPING EXACTLY AS PREDICTED IS ITSELF A RESULT: a written prediction of an ESCAPE landing is worth as much as a kill landing, because it says the disclosure is HONEST rather than a hedge.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE: V1 reds `xbit rev' ALONE; V6 reds `latch tick' AND `xbit rev'.  So `xbit rev' is not a pure collision detector and only `latch tick' separates the two causes -- the rows are COUPLED, the same shape already recorded for xbit isr / kill 20 wr txt, and a future reader pruning `redundant' rows would destroy the discrimination.  TABLE 5 IS RUNNING to re-verify V2 and V8 and to regression-check V1 (PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against 04:30:25).  *** KILL TABLE 5: THE CHAIN CLOSES.  EVERY PREDICTION LANDED, INCLUDING ONE PRE-REGISTERED AFTER PRETABLE5 WAS WRITTEN. ***  base 33/33 | V2_assigntick 32/33 (xbit rev2 -- THE ESCAPE CLOSED; it swept 32/32 clean before) | V8_len4 33/33 ESCAPED AS PREDICTED, now measured at the CORRECT cell | V1_assign20wr 32/33 (xbit rev ALONE).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against the table's 04:30:25.  *** THE DENOMINATOR, because a bare zero against a wrong enumeration does not close a chain -- this round supplied that counter-example itself at table 2. ***  TEN CELLS: {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} + {2 collision directions}.  Six closed before table 4; tables 4 and 5 killed the remaining four (V1/V2 the two collision directions, V4/V5 the two remaining latch-sets, V6 the tick latch-set).  TWO cells are DISCLOSED WITH A RULING and BOTH WERE MEASURED TO ESCAPE -- the counter (fix-independent, fable5) and the width axis at 0x10020 (outside the floor by the pcc2widthkills bound).  ZERO CELLS OUTSIDE THE ENUMERATION.  *** That is the fable5 fork's FIRST branch, so the chain CLOSES and the escape hatch -- drop the latch axis wholesale to disclosed debt -- REMAINS UNSPENT. ***  A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION THAT HELD, recorded before the result arrived: PRETABLE5 said V1 kills via `xbit rev' but did not say whether `xbit rev2' would ALSO red.  I derived beforehand that it would not -- under V1 the ISR assignment clobbers nothing because TICK was never set, the tick then sets its own bit, and the second ISR access is correctly silent.  MEASURED: `xbit rev' ALONE.  *** So the two collision rows separate BY DIRECTION, which is stronger than either row firing on both: the signature identifies WHICH clobber, not merely that one happened. ***  V1 at 32/33 (was 31/32 with one row red in table 4) also discharges the #462 regression check: adding `xbit rev2' did not perturb it.  That claim was REASONED in PRETABLE5 and is now MEASURED.  CONTRACT UPGRADED: the width disclosure now reads MEASURED TO ESCAPE rather than 'not swept'.  *** A disclosure backed by a MEASUREMENT tells a reader which edit walks free; one backed by an OMISSION only tells them nobody looked. ***  The incidental finding from the mis-built mutant is kept in the contract too: this detector DOES catch a width narrowing at imask0, with a four-row signature -- coverage on an arm the round never enumerated.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:49453:     "note": "*** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  `case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  `case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & (1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and `vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:50048:   "state_note": "*** AN UNBOUNDED, GUEST-TRIGGERABLE LOOP INSIDE A DEVICE ACCESS HANDLER.  THE EMULATOR NEVER DIES -- IT NEVER COMES BACK. ***  `maple_do_dma_xfer()` is `for (;;)` at `dev_dreamcast_maple.c:409`, and its ONLY exit is `:535` `if (last_message) break;` where `last_message = buf[3] & 0x80` (`:429`) is read **from guest RAM** at `d->dmaaddr`.  A guest that never sets bit 7 of that byte loops forever; `:532` only advances `addr`.  Triggered by an ordinary register write -- `case 0x18 MAPLE_STATE`, `case 1: maple_do_dma_xfer(cpu, d)` at `:588` -- after MAPLE_ENABLE and MAPLE_DMAADDR.  Machine: **dreamcast**. **THIS IS A THIRD CLASS AND THE TREE HAS NO NAME FOR IT.**  An `exit(`/`abort()` is a HOST DEATH; `cpu->running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT where the process lives and the strand stops. **This is neither: the process is alive, the strand is running, and the user's machine is gone.**  No sweep in this project looks for it, because there is no keyword -- which is exactly the property that let the `abort()` class survive three censuses. **NOT A DUPLICATE, verified:** `mapledmaaddr` is about the DMAADDR read value and `mapledmaadvance` about address advancement past the command.  Neither is the loop bound. There IS an `exit(1)` at `:426` inside the loop, on a different condition -- so the function has a terminating path and still has no trip cap. DISPOSITION NOT DECIDED HERE.  The obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop), but the bound is a design question: a legitimate maple chain has a real maximum and picking it needs the Comstedt reverse-engineering note the function header cites, not a guess.  **The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never a number that feels safe.**  *** THE BOUND IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THIS TREE, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN A FAILURE TO FIND ONE. ***  Comstedt's own DMA paragraph -- the page this file's header names -- says the controller *'will send ANY NUMBER of requests'*.  The 'configurable amount of time' in that sentence is **per request**, and it is ALREADY in the model as `d->timeout` (MAPLE_SPEED bits 16-31), **stored and never read inside `maple_do_dma_xfer`**.  On hardware a timeout writes 0xFFFFFFFF at the result address and CONTINUES to the next descriptor -- it does not end the list.  **`d->timeout` therefore cannot be turned into a frame cap without inventing a meaning the register does not have.** **24 IS THE ENDPOINT CENSUS, NOT THE LIST CEILING** (4 ports x 6 units): a legal DMA may send DEVINFO *and* GETCOND to the same unit, or a burst of VMU BWRITEs.  Linux's `MAPLE_MAXPACKETS 8` and the 16 KiB `MAPLE_DMA_SIZE` that Linux and KallistiOS both allocate are **SOFTWARE** batching, not ASIC limits, and neither is in this tree's model. *** AND IT CORRECTED THE DISPOSITION I WROTE INTO THIS ROW WHEN I FILED IT. ***  I wrote *'the obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop)'*.  **The seven-member option set is the WRONG MENU here: every member presumes an ACCESS with a value to refuse, and this is a LIST WALK whose only exit is a guest bit.**  Bounding the walk is a NEW member -- *'do not dress it up as member 3 applied to a loop and then pick 24'*. **THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT, GREPPED RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT DOES NOT TRANSFER:** `dev_sgi_mec.c`'s `for (;;)` IS bounded -- by `dma_ptr_nr >= 4` (the hardware has four DMA pointers) AND `j >= MAX_TX_PACKET_LEN`, and `#203` added the outer cap after an inner `break` left the outer loop writing past the array.  **But that bound comes from the HOST STORAGE the fill writes into, and maple writes GUEST RAM at `receive_addr` -- there is no host buffer to cap against.**  *And that answers the sighunt's open question: there is NO other `for (;;)` and no `while (1)` in `src/devices/*.c`; the sighunt's second one is mec, and mec is bounded.* **THE DISPOSITION, WITHOUT INVENTING N:** detect the **MISSING LAST**, not 'too many devices' -- the defect is a list that never terminates, not a list longer than 24.  **DECLINE THE WHOLE DMA**, which is the shape this same function already uses for a malformed DMA: the unaligned `addr & 0x1f` check prints and RETURNS with no `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT`.  *** TRUNCATING MID-LIST AND THEN FIRING DMADONE WOULD HAND THE GUEST A SILENT PARTIAL TRANSFER, and the unaligned path pointedly does not do that. ***  Latch the announce with `(once)` -- the unaligned path `fatal()`s EVERY time with no latch, which is floodclass.  **Do NOT `exit(1)`** (that converts a hang into a host death, the thing this queue is removing).  **Do NOT `cpu->running = 0`** (wrong class -- that is `lunafbhalt`/`8259cmdhalt`).  Do not make the DMA asynchronous in this round: that IS the hardware shape and would stop taking the host away, but it is a rewrite, and on real silicon a no-LAST list may still hang the Maple ASIC. **`:426` IS NOT A MODEL FOR MISSING LAST.**  All four `exit(1)`s in this function (`:426` GUN bit, `:459` multiple units, `:520` GETCOND function, `:529` unknown command) are UNIMPLEMENTED-PATH TRIPWIRES -- member 7's tell, a stub with no predicate refusing a value. *** BWRITE IS THE TELL: it is `fatal` + `break` OF THE SWITCH, and then the loop CONTINUES. So a no-LAST list of BWRITE HANGS while a no-LAST list of an unimplemented command DIES -- the same loop, two different classes, decided by `cmd`. ***  The author used `exit(1)` for 'I have not written this opcode', not for 'this list is malformed'. **Q4 -- IT CAN BE WITNESSED, AND AN EXISTING PROBE IS A LIVE HAZARD.**  Death-keyed oracles are all blind here: `exit(`, `abort()`, WIFSIGNALED and EOF-on-pty none of them fire.  *** AND `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py`'s `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` AFTER `rd(10.0)` WOULD BLOCK FOREVER ON THIS BUG -- that probe cannot be the detector, and if it ever reaches this path it hangs the harness rather than reporting anything. ***  The instrument is SIGKILL-after-deadline plus a LAST-set control; the enable and alignment checks both return before the loop, so they are the liveness controls. **WHAT WOULD SETTLE N:** measuring the real ASIC with lists of 24, 25 and 512 frames and one with LAST never set -- noting that on hardware Maple DMA is **ASYNCHRONOUS** (STATE=1 while in progress, the SH4 keeps running), so the question is what the ASIC does with no LAST, not what feels safe on the host.  **The synchronous modelling is itself part of why this presents as a host hang.**",
